export const HANDBOOK_TEXT = "Santa Cruz Cooperative School\n\nStudent / Family Handbook\n2025-26\nGrades 6 - 12\nDr. Casey Asato, Director General\nMr. Paul Petit, High School Principal\nMr. Hudson Thurston, Middle School Principal\nMrs. Clara Fernandez, Secondary Assistant Principal\n\nCalle Barcelona #1, Barrio Las Palmas\nSanta Cruz de la Sierra, Bolivia\nwww.sccs.edu.bo\n\n1. Introduction\n- Intro paragraph from Admin team that explains the purpose of it and that it guides all discipline\ndecisions\n- School Mission and Vision and Core Values\n- SCCS Learner Profile\n\n2. School Contact Information\n- Main Office Contact Information\n- Key Administrators and their Roles\n- School Counselor(s) Contact Information\n- Health Center Office Contact Information\n- Important Phone Numbers and Email Addresses\n- School Website and Social Media Links\n\n3. Academic Information\n- Daily School Schedule\n- Graduation Requirements\n- Advanced Placement Classes\n- Grading System\n- Homework Policies\n- Honor Roll Requirements\n- Senior Honor Roll Cords\n- Report Cards\n- Exchange programs and transfer credits\n- Valedictorian and Salutorian\n- Standardized testing\n- Bring Your Own Electronic Device Policy\n- SCCS AI guidelines\n- Grades and Academic Eligibility\n- Academic Support\n- Final Exams\n- Remediation Policies\n\n4. Attendance and Punctuality\n- School Attendance\n- Attendance Policy\n- Leaving Campus\n- Homework during absences\n-Withdrawal from school procedures\n- School Enrollment Certificate\n- Attendance at School Functions\n-Field trips/School Excursions\n\n5. School Policies and Regulations\n- Code of Conduct\n- Dress Code\n- Behavior at school assemblies\n- Public Display of Affection\n- Lockers and Backpacks\n- Hall Passes\n- Care of school grounds and property\n- Acoustic Pollution\n- Student Publications\n- Student’s Rights and Obligations\n- Classroom Violations\n- Phone Policy\n- Violations of Major School Rules and Procedures\n- Prevention Program\n- Academic Dishonesty\n- Test Taking Policy\n- Items not allowed on campus\n\n7. Extracurricular Activities\n- Extracurricular and Service Club Policy\n- Student Athlete’s Policy\n- Student Council\n- NJHS and NHS membership\n- Student Leadership Guidelines\n- Policy for Creating a new extracurricular club or activity\n\n8. Student Services\n- Health Services\n- Guest Speakers\n- Use of school telephone\n-Technology SCCS user policy\n- Cafeteria\n- Library regulations\n- Lost and Found\n- Sports Complex: procedures for use\n- Security for personal items\n\n9. Parent Involvement and Communication\n- Parent-Teacher Communication\n- Mother’s Club\n- Volunteering Opportunities\n- Student Led Conferences\n\n10. Transportation and Parking\n- Drop-off and Pick-up Procedures\n- Parking Regulations\n- Transportation for School Events\n\nLetter from Administration Team\nDear SCCS Community,\nWe wish you all the best as we start this school year. Our Jaguar Community is resilient, collaborative and\nrespectful which makes us confident that this will be a great school year of learning. We ask you all\n(parents, students and staff) to take some time and read the handbook thoroughly so that we all\nunderstand the rules, procedures and expectations that we have both in and outside of the classroom.\nAgain, we look forward to a great 2025-26 school year and remember, we are here to support you all.\nSCCS Administration\n\nSCCS Mission Statement:\nAt SCCS we cultivate lifelong learners who are critical and creative thinkers, principled leaders and\ncompassionate global citizens.\n\nSCCS Vision Statement:\nThe Jaguar community supports, inspires and challenges every mind and heart to reach their full\npotential.\n\nCore Values:\nRespect, Kindness, Integrity, Responsibility,\nResilience, Collaboration\n\nSCCS LEARNER PROFILE:\nSCCS students strive to be:\n● Inquirers, critical thinkers and problem solvers who cultivate curiosity, develop research skills,\nevaluate information critically, construct meaningful knowledge and apply it in the real world.\n● Collaborators and leaders with strong cultural, global and environmental awareness who develop\nstrong interpersonal and team-related skills, work autonomously and with others, learn from\neach other and contribute to the learning of others.\n● Effective communicators who express themselves confidently in more than one language and\nwho understand and are able to communicate ideas in writing, verbally and digitally under the\nconsideration of perspectives of other individuals and groups.\n● Creative and innovative learners who identify opportunities of scientific, economic and social\ngrowth and who independently and cooperatively explore and generate new ideas and strategies\nto become ambassadors of change.\n*Click Here to return to Top\n\nI\nImportant Contact Information\nSecondary Office: 591-3-353-0808\nDirector General: Dr. Casey Asato- casato@sccs.edu.bo\nSecondary Secretary: Mrs. Carlita Gorriti- cgorriti@sccs.edu.bo\nMiddle School Principal: Mr. Hudson Thurston- hthurston@sccs.edu.bo\nHigh School Principal: Mr. Paul Petit- ppetit@sccs.edu.bo\nSecondary Vice Principal: Mrs. Clara Fernandez- cfernandez@sccs.edu.bo\nHigh School Counselor: Ms. Gabriela Molina- gmolina@sccs.edu.bo\nMiddle School Counselor: Mrs. Maria Paula Aguirre- mpaguirre@sccs.edu.bo\nMarketing Admissions & Communications Director: Ms. Daniela Canessa- dcanessa@sccs.edu.bo\nSchool Doctor: Dra. Aneli Imopoco- healthcenter@sccs.edu.bo\n\nSocial Media/School Website\n\n🌐 Official Website\n\n● Santa Cruz Cooperative School: sccs.edu.bo\n\n📱 Social Media\n\n● LinkedIn: Santa Cruz Cooperative School (“sccsjaguars”) – Official school page\nyoutube.com+15linkedin.com+15facebook.com+15\n● Facebook: Santa Cruz Cooperative School (sccsjaguars) https://www.facebook.com/sccsjaguars\n● Instagram: @sccsjaguars – Visual stories, campus highlights, and daily snapshots from school life.\n\n🎥 YouTube\n\n● YouTube Channel: Santa Cruz Cooperative School – youtube.com+1youtube.com+1\n\n*Click Here to return to Top\n\nAcademic Overview\nSchedule\nStudents in grades 6-12 follow a block schedule of 55-minute periods (refer to the complete schedule at\nthe back of the booklet - Appendix A). Students will start school from Mondays through Fridays at 7:45\na.m. Academic classes will end at 03:10 p.m. every day; extracurricular classes will begin at 3:20 p.m. All\nstudents are scheduled for classes at all times. Some students may be scheduled into structured study\nhalls or the teaching aid community service program.\nAn alternative schedule is provided on a provisional basis for classes held using the virtual learning\nmodality. Virtual learning will be implemented under the Bolivian Government's recommendations and\ndecrees. SCCS will use Google Meets and Google Classroom as the official virtual learning platforms. Plus\nPortals will be used for recording of grades and attendance.\n\nMS Schedule\n\n7:45 8:40\n\nMONDAY\n\nTUESDAY\n\nWEDNESDAY\n\nTHURSDAY\n\nFRIDAY\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nB-BLOCK\n\nB-BLOCK\n\n8:45–\n9:10\n9:1510:10\n\nMS Break\nB-BLOCK\n\nB-BLOCK\n\n10:10-10\n:35\n\nB-BLOCK\nMS Advisory\n\n10:40-11\n:35\n\nC-BLOCK\n\nC-BLOCK\n\nC-BLOCK\n\nC-BLOCK\n\nC-BLOCK\n\n11:40 12:35\n\nD- BLOCK\n\nD- BLOCK\n\nD- BLOCK\n\nD- BLOCK\n\nD- BLOCK\n\n12:35-1:\n\nMS Lunch\n\n1:15 2:10\n\nE-BLOCK\n\nE-BLOCK\n\nE-BLOCK\n\nE-BLOCK\n\nE-BLOCK\n\n2:15 3:10\n\nF BLOCK\n\nF BLOCK\n\nF BLOCK\n\nF BLOCK\n\nF BLOCK\n\nHS Schedule\n\n7:45 8:40\n\nMONDAY\n\nTUESDAY\n\nWEDNESDAY\n\nTHURSDAY\n\nFRIDAY\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nA-BLOCK\n\nB-BLOCK\n\nB-BLOCK\n\n8:45–\n9:10\n9:1510:10\n\nHigh School Advisory\nB-BLOCK\n\nB-BLOCK\n\n10:10-10\n:35\n\nB-BLOCK\nHigh School Break\n\n10:40-11\n:35\n\nC-BLOCK\n\nC-BLOCK\n\nC-BLOCK\n\nC-BLOCK\n\nC-BLOCK\n\n11:40 12:35\n\nD- BLOCK\n\nD- BLOCK\n\nD- BLOCK\n\nD- BLOCK\n\nD- BLOCK\n\n12:40\n-1:35\n\nE-BLOCK\n\nE-BLOCK\n\nE-BLOCK\n\nE-BLOCK\n\nE-BLOCK\n\n1:35 2:15\n\nLunch\n\nLunch\n\nLunch\n\nLunch\n\nLunch\n\n2:15 3:10\n\nF-BLOCK\n\nF-BLOCK\n\nF-BLOCK\n\nF-BLOCK\n\nF-BLOCK\n\nGRADUATION REQUIREMENTS\n• The Santa Cruz Cooperative School, SCCS, is accredited by COGNIA (formerly AdvancED), and is\nrecognized by the Bolivian Ministry of Education. SCCS's academic program is college-preparatory,\ndesigned to prepare students for entry into colleges and universities in the United States, Latin America\nand virtually any other country in the world.\n• Programs of study lead to two different diplomas: The American Diploma (equivalent to a US Diploma\nissued by accredited High Schools in the United States) and the Bolivian “Bachillerato en Humanidades”,\nrequired for entry into a Bolivian or other Latin American Universities.\n• All SCCS students and transferring students, starting the 7th grade, are to pursue the Bolivian and the\nAmerican High School Diploma. Students who transfer to SCCS from educational institutions in foreign\ncountries need to provide all academic records acknowledged by the Bolivian Embassy of the respective\nhost country in order to be granted the recognition of credits for the Bolivian and American High School\nDiploma. All students at SCCS must be enrolled in the Bolivian Diploma Program and the US Diploma\nProgram.\n• The subjects offered at all levels cover respective graduation requirements for both Diplomas and may\nnot be dropped.\n• Specific graduation ceremony procedures are outlined in the SCCS Graduation Handbook.\nSpecific credit requirements for the American (grades 9-12) and Bolivian Diploma (earned in grades\n7-12):\n\nAmerican High School Diploma Bolivian High School Diploma\n\nEnglish:\n4 credits\n\nSpanish/Native Language:4 credits\nMathematics: 3 credits\n\nMathematics: 5 credits\nSocial Studies*:3 credits\n\nSocial Studies: 6.5 credits\nScience:\n3 credits\n\nBiology/Geography: 4 credits\nForeign Language: 2 credits\nPhysics/Chemistry: 3.5 credits\nPhysical Education 2 credits\nForeign Language: 2 credits\nSTEAM:\n1.0 credit\nPsychology/Philosophy: 2 credits\nSenior Project: 0.25 credits\nPhysical Education: 2 credits\nCareer Planning 0.5 credits\nEthics / Morality: 2 credits\nElectives:\n6 credits\nArt / Music: 4 credits\n\nScience, Technology, Production: 6 credits\n\nTotals:\n24.75 credits\nTotals:\n41 Credits\n* must include World History I/II, US History, US Government.\nImportant: For the completion of the US High School Diploma credits are based on a predetermined\nnumber of hours (1 credit = 120 hours of study).\nThe courses Career Planning and Senior Independent Project are considered graduation requirements.\nEven if a student does not apply for college in an English speaking country,he/she needs to take these\ncourses. Only students that are on a specialized study plan (SSP)may be exempt from this requirement.\nSCCS commits to offering an SAT preparation opportunity annually to students preparing for the exam.\n\nAdvanced Placement Program / Advanced Placement Policy\nSCCS supports motivated students in pursuing the rigor of Advanced Placement (AP) classes that offer\ncollege-level curricula designed to help prepare students for a successful university experience. Not only\ncan students gain a greater understanding of the material but can potentially earn college credit based on\ntheir score on the external AP exam given in May. Even if students do not pass the AP exam, they will\nhave a real idea of what to expect when you attend university.\nSCCS has an open enrollment policy for the AP courses offered. SCCS believes that high school students\ninterested in a better pre-college education should consider challenging themselves by enrolling in AP\ncourses. Students may apply to any AP course offered, as long as they fulfill entrance qualifications.\n\nAP Course Selection Process\n● Interested students should meet the following requirements:\n■ A minimum year average grade of 85% on a 100% scale in the relevant subject(s)\nfrom the previous year.\n■ A relevant MAP test score at or above the 60th percentile score.\n■ A PSAT score at or above the 60th percentile score.\n■ Recommendation by the College Board to take the respective subject (College\nBoard AP Potential Report).\n● Students not meeting the above requirements can apply for an exception by doing the\nfollowing:\n■ Get one teacher recommendation recommending the student for the AP subject.\n● The limit for the number of AP courses a student can take is based on their grade level as\nfollows:\n■ 10th grade - 2 AP courses.\n■ 11th & 12th grade - 3 AP courses.\nAny student wishing to take 4 AP courses in 11th or 12th grade must have passed 2 or\nmore AP courses (taken concurrently) in the previous year with a minimum score of 4\n(College Board).\n● Applicants to an AP course are expected to complete any summer assignments. Failure to\ncomplete these Pre-AP assignments will lead to removal from the AP Course.\n● Any special circumstances (as determined by the AP coordinator) will be discussed in a\nmeeting with the administration.\n● Applicants will receive an acceptance notification.\n\nAP Grading Policy\nStudents who participate in AP classes have a significantly heavier workload. In addition to\nincreased rigor and nightly assignments, most AP students are required to complete\nassignments during the vacation and if not turned in on the due date will be automatically\nremoved from the class. Grading in AP courses is more rigorous than in common high school\ncourses. Due to the additional workload AP classes are weighted and receive an additional 5%\n\non each semester report card grade for students who earn an unweighted grade of at least\n70%.\nStudents will not be awarded the 5% academic honor points if:\n- They decide to not enroll for the AP exam\n- They decide to not take the AP exam\n- They have more than 10 absences in the semester in the respective AP class. This\nrefers to unexcused and excused absences and includes AP students who are student\nathletes or have other external commitments that lead to long-term absences.\n\nAP Course Commitment\nPlease be aware there are strict requirements and prerequisites for AP courses. There are specific\nrestrictions in order to drop an AP course as stated below. All students enrolled in AP courses will be\nexpected to take the exam in May. Students have made a year-long commitment and must be willing to\nwork to be successful. This is why students and parents must weigh all factors before choosing to take\nthese courses. We strongly encourage all students to challenge themselves and take their academics\nseriously. None of the above mentioned information is meant to discourage students, but rather assist\nthem in making a well-informed decision that is best suited for the student and family.\n\nAP Dropping Policy\n● Students admitted to an AP course have the option to drop the program by the\nfirst-quarter progress report. Dropping an AP course at a later date is not permitted.\n● In case of withdrawal from an AP course, the grade earned will be transferred as an\nofficial course grade to the academic program that replaces the AP course.\n\nAP Exam Registrations\nAccording to College Board regulations the enrollment and exam ordering process closes on November 1\nof the respective calendar year. AP students who are planning to take AP exams in May will have to pay\nthe respective exam fee which can fluctuate due to the floating exchange rate per exam before October\n15 at the SCCS Finance Office. All AP students are required to present the Exam Registration Form,\ntogether with the payment confirmation slip, with the High School Principal/AP Coordinator by no later\nthan October 15.\n\nAP Course Offerings 2025-26\nThe Pre-AP English curriculum is offered in grades 9 and 10. All High School level mathematics courses\nfollow a Pre- AP curriculum. AP course offerings at SCCS include:\nAP World History (in grade 10)\nAP Spanish Language and Composition (in grade 10)\nAP Spanish Literature and Composition (in grade 12)\nAP English Language and Composition (in grade 11)\nAP English Literature and Composition (in grade 12)\nAP Pre Calculus (in grades 11/12)\n\nAP Calculus AB (in grade 12)\nAP Macroeconomics (for grade 11/12)\nAP Chemistry (for grades 11/12)\nAP Statistics ( for grades 11 and 12)\nAP courses must have a minimum of 8 students.\nStudents can take other AP exams as an independent study. The costs for independent study courses are\nto be covered by the student. Independent AP courses at authorized online academies will be added to\nthe student’s academic record and grades will be transferred to report cards and student transcript; AP\nonline progress reports must be presented to the Principal before SCCS progress report and report card\ndates.\n\nGRADING SYSTEM\nAcademic credit is awarded based upon final grades. In a yearlong course, the average of the first and\nsecond-semester grades determine the final grade. High School semester grades are calculated by\nassigning 42.5% value to each quarter and 15% to the final exam. Middle School final averages are based\non the average of each quarter.\nA numerical grading system is used in middle school and in high school. Grades in the 90 – 100 range\nrepresent superior performance, 80 – 89 above average, 70 – 79 average, 60 – 69 below average, and\nbelow 60 failing (no credit). Official Bolivian documents use a 100% grading scale as well. The Middle\nSchool report cards will show the grade point average taken from their core academic classes only\n(English, Spanish, science, social studies and math). The American transcript will use numerical grades\nbased on the percentage scale only. Official High School Transcripts will show the overall GPA, the average\nof all academic subjects taken in one school year. No grades will be awarded for the student participation\nin advisory classes or individual learning classes.\n6th-8th Grading Policy\nAcademic evaluation in middle school works on a points system (no weighted categories):\nMajor Assessments / Projects / Research Papers: 100pts\nQuizzes / Minor Projects / Labs: 50 pts\nHomework /Classwork / In-class participation: 10 pts\nEnglish:\nSummative Assessments: 2 (must include 1 major writing assignment)\nMinor Writing Assignment or Quizzes: 4\nHomework/ Class work /Participation Grades: 12\nMath:\nSummative Assessments: 2\nQuizzes: 4\nHomework: most classes (20 plus)\n\nSocial Studies:\nSummative Assessments: 2\nQuizzes: 4\nHomework/ Classwork: 10\nScience:\nSummative Assessments: 2\nQuizzes: on Labs / Vocabulary / General Knowledge: 3\nClass work /Homework: 12\nLabs / Projects: hands on activity including data collection and analysis: 2\nSTEAM:\nSummative Assessments: 2\nQuizzes: 3\nHomework assignments: 5\nClass assignments: 6\nSpanish:\nSummative Assessments: 2 (must include 1 major writing assignment)\nMinor Writing Assignment or Quizzes: 4\nHomework/ Classwork /Participation Grades: 10\n\nHigh School Assessment Policy\nStudents and teachers work in partnership to allow students to demonstrate what they have learned,\nassess their progress, and identify specific strategies to maximize their potential on internal and external\nassessments. Teachers use both formative and summative assessment to inform students of their learning\nand progress.\n● Formative assessment refers to the ongoing process students and teachers engage in when they:\n○ Focus on learning goals;\n○ Take stock of where current work is in relation to the goal;\n○ Take action to move closer to the goal.\nSummative assessments are used to evaluate student learning, skill acquisition, and academic\nachievement at the conclusion of a defined instructional period—typically at the end of a learning\nsegment, unit, course, semester, program, or school year. Summative assessments are the teacher’s final\nopportunity to assess the summation of learning before moving on to new topics or skills.\nIn an attempt to assist students in balancing their daily study schedule, the high school employs an\nassessment schedule that allows for no more than two major assessments to be given or due on any\ngiven day. As a general guideline, major assessments are defined as tests, quizzes, essays, or projects that\nconstitute more than 10% of the quarter grade and/or take more than 20 minutes of class time. Quizzes\nthat are shorter than 20 minutes and daily homework may be assigned or due on any day or block.\nTeachers may have to make exceptions to accommodate AP assessment.\nThe school also recognizes the need for students to have time to catch up with work and to spend quality\ntime with family, therefore major assessments due the day after a holiday break should be avoided.\n\nHigh School Grading Policy\nAcademic evaluation in high school is based on weighted categories published to the students and\nparents. It is, however, mandatory for all students to complete the following minimum assignments in\ncore subjects on a weekly or quarter basis (English, Math, Spanish, Social Studies and Science):\n● Minimum of two assignments per academic week (reported on Plus Portal by on Friday of each\nweek).\n● Minimum of Summative per academic quarter.\n● Summative tests (45 min) are permitted only once per academic week.\n● Tests may not be assigned as a conduct consequence. Teachers may require students to re-take an\nassessment outside of the normal class schedule for students' low performance on any\nassessment.\n\nLate Work Policy (Grades 6-12)\nProjects, major assignments and other out-of-class assignments will have 20 percent of the grade\ndeducted from the final grade for every school day that it is late. No assignments will be accepted after\nthe 3rd day and will receive a 25% as a grade.\nExample: Assignment is due Monday: Monday - worth 100 pts/percent; Tuesday - worth 80 pts/percent;\nWednesday - worth 60 pts/percent; Thursday – worth 25 pts/percent.\nIf a student is absent for medical reasons (documented medical certificate required), educational reasons\n(workshops, external exams, etc.) or athletic reasons (tournaments, Friendship Games, etc.), a\n1-day-make-up time frame is allowed for each day missed with a maximum of 3 days without discount\nin points / percentage. In cases of severe sickness or hospitalization an individual plan can be made by\nthe teachers and administration which would allow ample time for the student to make up any missed\nwork.\nIf a student is absent for personal reasons (trips, personal business, etc.), a 3-day-make-up frame is given\nand work is to be presented the latest on the third day upon return. Work presented after the 3rd day\nupon return will not be accepted. Late work as a result of unexcused absences for personal reasons or\nany academic evaluation administered during the time of a personal absence (test, quiz, project), will\nbe graded based on the late work policy of daily deductions listed above.\nStudents must check Google Classroom and emails regularly to stay informed about tests, projects and\ntasks assigned during the student’s absence.\nFor the officially established report card dates (1st quarter, 1st semester, 3rd quarter, 2nd semester) the\nextension of deadlines is not possible. Students with long-term absences due to tournaments or other\nauthorized trips must have end of grading period-related work assignments completed prior to the\ndeadline. Exceptions can only be made for severe medical circumstances such as hospitalization, surgery,\netc.\n\nSecondary Homework Policy\nHomework assignments are considered essential at SCCS. Homework assignments should be relevant and\nmeaningful and serve to help students to develop good study habits, to reinforce and/or strengthen skills\nand academic concepts, to encourage independent learning and student responsibility, to provide guided\nreading opportunities, to complete research activities and/or to work on reports and long-term projects.\nGroup projects should not be assigned as homework or worked on without teacher supervision. Students\nlearn to pace themselves and manage time. It is reasonable to expect homework assignments on each\nschool day.\nCORE SUBJECTS:\nGrade 6-8\nEnglish 6-8\n\nCORE SUBJECTS:\nGrade 9\nEnglish 9\n\nCORE SUBJECTS:\nGrade 10\nEnglish 10\n\nCORE SUBJECTS:\nGrade 11\nEnglish / AP Engl.\n\nMath 6-8\nSocial Studies 6-8\n\nGeometry / Alg. I\nIndiv. & Societies\nHuman Rights\nScience 9\nSpanish 9\n\nGeometry / Alg. II\nWorld History\n\nPrecalc / Alg. II\nUS History\n\nLife Science / Coding\nAP Span. Lang.\nSpanish 10\nAP World History\nPsychology / Coding\n\nPhysical Science\nSpanish 11\n\nLimited\nHOMEWORK:\nArt 10 / Band\nPE 10\n\nLimited\nHOMEWORK:\nFitness 11/12\nElective\n\nCORE SUBJECTS:\nGrade 12\nEngl. 12 /\nHonors/AP Engl.\nPrecalc /Calculus\nUS Gov./\nEconomics\nPhysics II\nSpan 12 / AP\nSpan. Literature\nAP Chemistry\nAP Statistics\nAP Macroecon.\nLimited\nHOMEWORK:\nCareer Planning\nElective\n\nElectives\n\nElectives\n\nElectives\n\nScience 6-8\nSpanish 6-8\n\nLimited\nHOMEWORK:\nArt 6-8\nPE 6-8\nSTEAM 6-8\nBand/Music\n2nd Step/Academy\n\nLimited\nHOMEWORK:\nPublic Speaking\nArt / Band /\nTheater\nPE 9\nElective\n\nAP Chemistry\nAP Statistics\n\nHonor Roll / Academic Recognitions\nSecondary Honor Roll will be published to parents and students at the end of each academic semester.\nHonor Roll recognition in 6th-8th grades is based on academic average in core subjects only, while honor\nRoll recognition in high school is also based on the academic average in core subjects only. Some special\nrecognitions may consider the overall GPA. There are three Honor Roll categories at each grade level.\nQualifying standards are as follows:\nHigh Honor Roll: minimum average of 95%, with no grade below 85%\nHonor Roll: minimum average of 90%, with no grade below 75% and only one C-grade.\nA student who has one or more behavior observations on the discipline record or who surpasses the\nallowable number of absences and / or tardies will not be eligible for the Honor Roll.\n\nIf a student in middle or high school has twenty (20) or more tardies or 3 or more unexcused absences\nin a semester, he/she will not be eligible for any academic or leadership recognition (Honor Roll,\nPresidential Awards, NJHS, NHS, STUCO, etc.).\n\nSenior Honor Cords\nSenior Honor Cords for Graduation will be awarded after a continuous participation of four years (9-12)\nfor the areas of: Community Service and Clubs, Honor Roll, Fine Arts Participation and Athletic\nParticipation (3 years). The NHS sash will be awarded to NHS members. Seniors are expected to submit\nthe High School Achievement Record Form at the end of the first semester of the senior year in order to\nbe considered for Honor Cord Recognitions. Record Forms received after this due date cannot be\nconsidered for cord recognition. Transfer students who provide a record of continuous\n4-year-performance in a designated area may be considered for honor cords reception. To be awarded a\nsenior cord for the Honor Roll at SCCS, a student must have received an honor roll recognition in at least 6\nout of the 7 semesters from the start of grade 9 until the end of the first semester in senior year.\nParticipation in activities receiving cords will be verified by the faculty and administration.\n\nCollege Counseling\nSCCS provides a college counselor who helps guide and assist our students, beginning in grade 11, in\ntheir college search. You can read a comprehensive description of our college counseling program here.\n\nReport Cards\nParents and students are informed of the level of student achievement in each of the student’s courses by\nmeans of a report card issued at the end of each quarter and each semester. The semester grades and\nfinal averages are reported on official transcripts for students from grades 6-12. Report Cards are official\nschool documents; report card copies are to be signed by all parents. Report cards can not be picked up\nby any parent who has debt with the school.\n\nProgress of Grades\nParents are encouraged to check plusportals weekly so that they are aware of their child’s grade in each\nclass. Teachers are expected to update grades on plusportals at least once per week.\n\nIncomplete Grades\nWhen a student has not completed work assigned due to illness or another emergency, a teacher may\nreport a grade of incomplete (INC). A valid medical certificate or other documentation will be required by\nthe school. Family trips or other personal reasons of absence will not be taken into consideration for the\ncompletion of unfinished course work. Incomplete work must be completed within the time designated\nby the principal or the student’s final semester grade will be calculated using a failing grade for the\nquarter.\n\nChange of Grades\nIf a student notices a mistake on a particular grade on the official progress report or report card, it is the\nfamily and student’s responsibility to approach the subject-specific teacher and request grade\nmodification. This request must be presented within two weeks after the report card/progress report\ndistribution. Grade modification requests presented after this timeframe will not be authorized. The\nteacher will verify the appeal and request authorization for grade change from the respective division\nprincipal. A new progress report or report card will be made available to the parents within three days\nafter authorization of grade change.\n\nExchange Programs and Transfer Credits\nStudents who are planning to participate in an exchange program are required to complete the\nTemporary Student Withdrawal Form at the SCCS Admissions Office requesting approval from their\nrespective administrator. A copy of the withdrawal form must be presented to the division principal and\nacademic recommendations on subject-specific requirements at the exchange school will be given to the\nparents.\nAlthough SCCS accepts transfer credits toward the fulfillment of the diploma requirements, these credits\nare not integrated into the SCCS system. In case of the student's participation in a study-abroad or\nexchange program transfer credits will be counted towards graduation and included on the high school\ntranscript. Grades from the partner school will not be placed on SCCS official transcripts, It is the\nobligation of the parent to legalize all academic records from non-Bolivian educational institutions\nthrough the respective embassies prior to admission or readmission to SCCS.\n\nValedictorian and Salutatorian\nThe student from senior class who at the end of the third academic quarter of the senior year has\nachieved the highest cumulative GPA in all subjects during grades 9-12 receives the recognition of\n\"Valedictorian\". The senior with the second-highest cumulative GPA is recognized as the \"Salutatorian\". In\norder to be able to receive the recognition of \"valedictorian\" or \"salutatorian\" the student must have\nearned credits at SCCS during at least six semesters.\n\nStandardized Testing\nAll SCCS secondary students are required to perform standardized tests at various times throughout the\nyear. These tests, including the WrAP (ERB) and SCCS subject-specific Writing Prompts (grades 6 – 12),\nMAP (grades 6-10) and PSAT (grades 9-11), SAT (grades 11-12) and TOEFL (grade 12), assess skills in critical\nreading, writing, mathematics and science. Test scores will be provided to parents upon request through\nthe Principal’s office. Due to the importance of ERB and MAP testing at SCCS, individual score reports will\nbe attached to the first semester and final report cards. All students that are considered below the\nestablished writing standard for each grade level (WrAP) may be required to participate in a writing\nreinforcement process and remediation sessions after school or during the academic winter camp. An\nassessment grade will be awarded for the WrAP standardized test performance in English. Students with\nabsences during the times of standardized testing are responsible for scheduling alternative test dates for\nthe respective standardized test. If alternative testing is not possible (due to pre-established dates of\ntesting windows), absent students will lose the opportunity to be assessed by external testing agencies.\n\nSCCS covers the cost for the following standardized examinations: PSAT in grades 9, 10 and 11, MAP and\nthe WrAP (ERB). Participation in the scheduled exam sessions is mandatory for all students at the\nrespective grade levels.\nStudents in grades 11 and 12 are encouraged to take at least one SAT exam per school year.\n\nBring Your Own Device Policy\nAt SCCS, the Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) program empowers secondary students with the tools\nnecessary to excel in a technology-driven academic environment. To ensure a smooth experience as we\ntransition to the BYOD system for students, parents, and staff, the following guidelines apply:\n\nGeneral Guidelines\n1. Device Responsibility: Students are solely responsible for the care and maintenance of their\npersonal devices. The school’s technology department will not provide repair services or\ntechnical support for personal devices.\n2. Daily Requirements: Students must bring their devices to school fully charged and ready to use\nfor academic activities. We will not lend any student a charger.\n3. Appropriate Use: Devices must only be used for school-related tasks during class. Unauthorized\nuse (e.g., gaming or social media) during instructional time is prohibited.\n4. Accountability: Students are expected to handle their devices responsibly and follow the\nschool’s Acceptable Use Policy.\n\nMinimum Device Specifications\nA student may continue to use their school-purchased Chromebook if it is still working well. To ensure\ncompatibility with academic and testing requirements (NWEA, Bluebook, AP Testing), student devices\nmust meet the following minimum specifications:\n●\n●\n●\n●\n\n●\n●\n●\n●\n\nProcessor: Intel i5 or AMD Ryzen 5 (or higher)\nRAM: 8 GB minimum\nStorage: 256 GB SSD (Solid-State Drive) recommended\nOperating System:\n○ Windows 10 (or later)\n○ macOS 12 Monterey (or later)\n○ Chromebooks with access to secure browser support for testing platforms\nScreen Size: 11-inch minimum display\nBattery Life: 6+ hours to support a full school day\nConnectivity: Dual-band Wi-Fi (2.4 GHz and 5 GHz support)\nAccessories: Keyboard, mouse/trackpad, and headphones with a microphone\n\nTesting Readiness\n1. Devices must be capable of running secure testing software such as:\n○ NWEA (For MAP) Secure Browser\n\n○ Bluebook for digital AP exams\n○ Other testing platforms as required by SCCS\n2. It is the student’s responsibility to ensure their device meets software compatibility\nrequirements. Regular updates must be installed to maintain functionality.\n3. Students may be required to download and install a lockdown browser required by the school.\n\nTech Department Guidelines\n1. The school’s technology department will assist students in accessing the school’s Wi-Fi network\nand troubleshooting connectivity issues.\n2. The department will not repair, upgrade, or provide technical assistance for hardware or\nsoftware issues on personal devices.\n3. If a device fails to meet school requirements, students will be required to rent or purchase a\nschool-approved device.\n\nParent and Student Agreement\n1. Parents and students must review the SCCS BYOD Agreement Form, acknowledging their\nunderstanding of and compliance with this policy.\n2. Any misuse of personal devices or failure to meet device requirements may result in disciplinary\naction or restrictions on device use at school.\n3. If students opt to continue with a Chromebook, they will still be responsible for their repair and\nmaintenance.\n\nSCCS AI Guidelines\n1. Teachers\na. Teachers will communicate openly with students about AI’s role in learning.\ni. For example, if AI tools like ChatGPT are allowed for idea generation but not for\nfinal essays, this should be clarified at the start of the course.\nii. Teachers should create levels of AI use (Level 1-No AI, Level 2- idea generation is\nallowed, Level 3- specific parts must be AI generated). If not allowed, teachers\nmust clearly state that for the assignment.\niii. Assignments will include clear expectations for AI use, such as citing AI tools or\ndemonstrating how they contributed to the work.\nb. Discussions about AI will be integrated into lessons to help students critically understand\nhow AI collects, processes, and presents information (it can be as simple as the probable\nuse and misuse of AI in a specific lesson).\nc. Teachers will guide students in the ethical use of AI and emphasize its role as a creative\nand critical thinking tool rather than a shortcut for completing assignments.\nd. Teachers will actively participate in training to stay updated on AI applications in\neducation and share insights with peers to ensure effective classroom integration.\ne. Teachers may flag AI-generated student output and must validate through a\nconversation with the student. School rules will apply once the student is unable to\nfurther discuss nor defend that the output was his own.\n\n2. Students\na. Students will be responsible for using AI ethically. For instance, disclosing and citing any\nAI tool used in assignments, such as including an “AI Credits” section that details its role\nin brainstorming or editing.\nb. Plagiarism rules apply to AI use. Submitting AI-generated content as one’s own work is\nconsidered academic misconduct. For example, using ChatGPT to write an entire essay\neven with AI acknowledgement is not allowed.\nc. Students are only allowed to use AI tools (provided that the teacher indicated) for\nbrainstorming and enhancing ideas but must balance this with their independent\nthinking. For example, AI-generated outlines can be a starting point, but final work\nshould reflect personal effort. (within class, after pre-brainstorming, no electronics,\nteacher permission, previous discussion with teacher for clarity)\nd. Privacy is critical. Students should use AI tools approved by the school to avoid sharing\nsensitive information with unverified platforms.\ne. Students will participate in lessons on ethical AI use at the beginning of the school year\n(per grade level), including identifying bias in AI outputs and understanding how AI\nsystems reflect the perspectives of their creators. (digital citizenship classes K-12)\nf. Students should respond to flagged AI-generated work by discussing his process in\ncreating their own work, etc.\n\n3. Parents\na. Support your child in ethical AI use by encouraging critical thinking and creativity. For\nexample, discuss how AI can assist but should not replace personal effort.\nb. Partner with the school to ensure your child’s data is protected and that AI tools meet\nprivacy standards.\nc. Parents are encouraged to stay informed about the school’s AI initiatives and ask\nquestions about how AI is shaping learning experiences.\nd. Participate in a Parent University Training (required) on the evolving role of AI in\neducation to better understand its benefits and limitations for your child’s development.\n(student work won’t be considered if parent has not attended this training)\ni. Parents will be invited to workshops like \"Discover the World of AI\" to learn\nabout AI’s role in education and engage with its applications in school. (Data\nprotection, privacy, and digital blueprint.)\nii.\n4. Administration\na. Administrators will identify annual training/PD for teachers on AI integration, focusing\non ethical usage and improving learning outcomes.\nb. Remind teachers about the dangers of exposing students’ identities in using AI.\nc. Must intervene in cases that there is a dispute in AI-generated work.\nd. AI applications will be revisited regularly to assess their impact on equity and student\nlearning to address any risks or gaps. (specific to the tech department).\ne. Administrators, together with the tech coordinator should indicate what are the\nschool-approved AI tools that will not compromise privacy.\n\nf. With the help of the Tech Coordinator, tools and systems will be implemented with\nrobust privacy and data protection measures, to ensure compliance with ethical\nguidelines.\n\nTextbooks\nStudents are expected to take care of all issued books. Lost or damaged books must be paid for at the\nreplacement rate cost. Textbooks are to be covered by the student upon receipt.\n\nGrades and Athletic Eligibility\nStudents are eligible to participate in any of the SCCS Athletic Teams as long as their GPA is higher than\n70% on a 100% scale. Students are not allowed to have an F-grade on the designated cutoff dates in order\nto participate in any local and/or national tournaments, including Friendship Games. Cutoff dates are: first\nquarter report card for first semester athletic events and third quarter report date for athletic events in\nthe second semester. Grades that were improved after the cutoff dates will not be considered.\nFurthermore, students must demonstrate satisfactory behavior in school to be eligible to participate.\nStudents with excessive absences (more than 3 unexcused absences per quarter) and tardies (10 tardies\nper quarter) will not be eligible to participate in athletics until a new quarter begins. Students on a\nbehavior agreement will not be eligible to join any athletic team.\n\nAfter-school support\nIf a student has difficulties in a subject, after-school help (ongoing remediation) with the teacher is\navailable from 3:15-4:00 p.m. on Tuesdays and Thursdays. In after-school sessions, teachers will answer\nspecific questions a student may have. Teachers may also use the time for students to make-up work\nfrom excused absences, study for tests and quizzes, or answer questions that were not asked in class.\nParticipation in the ongoing remediation program is mandatory and takes priority over all other\nafter-school activities. If a student does not attend remediation classes, parents will be notified and the\nabsence will be registered on the remedial attendance record which constitutes official documentation\nfor possible retention.\nStudents who have a grade of 59 or below in any other subject must attend remediation sessions for\nthe entire academic quarter. If a student is scheduled into after-school support classes due to a low or\nfailing grade, he/she will temporarily not be able to participate in any other extracurricular activity on the\nday(s) of remediation class(es). Students assigned to remediation must attend remedial afternoon\nclasses until the end of the respective academic quarter.\n\nTutoring\nIn a few cases, students may need reinforcement in certain areas beyond what can be covered in class or\nin after-school help sessions. External tutoring support is considered a private matter but only in\nextreme necessity as students should pay attention in class and attend SCCS after school help. On\noccasions, the administration may suggest an external tutor. Tutoring support is recommended in case of\nacademic difficulties only, but not for homework completion. All tutoring for pay must take place off\ncampus. Teachers are not allowed to tutor their own students for pay. The secondary administration\ncan recommend qualified professionals for tutoring.\n\nEarly Final Examinations\nFinal examinations are such an integral and important part of a course that it is school policy that they\nare taken only during regularly scheduled times. Exceptions are made only when student illness\n(documented by a physician’s statement). Requests for exceptions to the school’s policy must be made in\nwriting to the respective Principal well in advance (at least 2 weeks) of exams. If necessary, final\nmake-up exams must be taken within one week upon the start of the second semester. It is the\nresponsibility of the student to schedule make-up final examinations with the division principal. If a\nstudent is absent for these end-of-semester examinations due to reasons other than the stated above, a\nfailing grade (a zero) will be awarded for the value of the final exam.\n\nExam Exonerations\nStudents in grades 9-12 may be exempt from second semester exams if they maintain an accumulative\naverage of 97% or higher in a subject through to the end of the fourth quarter. To be exonerated,\nstudents must maintain an acceptable attendance record (no more than 3 days of unexcused absences\nduring the school year and no more than 10 tardies per school year in each subject). No exam\nexonerations will be considered for first semester final exams. No exam exemption is considered for\nsemester courses. Students who fulfill the requirements for exam exoneration will be exempt from\nend-of-year final examinations, but not from final projects. No exam exonerations will be considered\nfor final exams in AP courses. No exam exonerations will be applied, if the final exam is project-based.\n\nCourses Failed\nAt SCCS a student will have to repeat the school year if he/she shows failing grades in 4 or more\nacademic subjects.\nThe following procedures will apply for students who fail courses that are required for earning the\nAmerican Diploma at SCCS.\nA student in grades 9-12 who fails a required course must enroll in an accredited American High School\n(other than SCCS) or correspondence school (Ex. University of Nebraska Online Study High School, VHS\nLearning online) and earn the appropriate passing credit within the discipline of the course that the\nstudent failed. Upon successful completion of the course, the student will be given credit for the course\nwhich will show up on their transcript. The grade earned on the recovery course will not be included on\nthe SCCS transcript.\nThe cost of the correspondence class and any associated materials is the sole responsibility of the student\nand his / her parents. Students who fail academic courses are required to attend the two-week academic\nwinter camp at the end of the school year where online assignments will be completed.\nSCCS will follow the guidelines of COGNIA, our accrediting agency, in the granting of all credits.\nA student who has failed a course that is a requirement for the American Diploma will be offered\nongoing remediation courses throughout the school year and will participate in our Winter Camp as\nlong as he/she does not fail more than three courses.\n\nNote: The following procedures will apply for students who fail courses which are required to earn the\nBolivian Bachillerato at SCCS:\nA student in grades 6 - 12 who is failing a required course will be assigned to the remediation program,\nafter school, as additional support for their children before engaging in external tutoring. The remediation\nprogram is offered throughout the year (ongoing remediation). During remediation, students will receive\nan opportunity to develop skills and knowledge in areas that went undeveloped during the corresponding\nquarter and to complete work that has yet to be completed and passed in for assessment. Students will\nbe allowed to exit the on-going remediation program at the end of the respective academic quarter, once\nthey have displayed a passing grade in the course.\nAny student in grades 6 - 12 who fails a required course with a grade below 60 percent will be required to\nattend Winter Camp. Any student, who scores less than an average of 51% in any single one of the\nsubject areas, will have to repeat the school year, as described in Article 35 of the Manual of Curricular\nEvaluation (Bolivian Ministry of Education).\n\nRepeating the School Year in Secondary\nIf a student fails four or more academic subjects required for the US Diploma after remediation with a\nfinal grade below 60%, he or she will be required to repeat the entire year.\nIf a student fails any subject required for the Bolivian Diploma after remediation during the school year\nwith a final grade lower than 51%, he/she will be required to repeat the entire year.\n*Click Here to return to Top\n\nSTUDENT ATTENDANCE\nAn excellent attendance record allows students to maximize their individual academic potential. When\nstudents are absent or late to school or class, not only does their learning suffer significantly, but the\nlearning of other students is also affected. Therefore, we expect students to be in school and to be on\ntime for classes. Class attendance expectations apply to all three learning modalities at SCCS: on-campus,\nhybrid and virtual learning. For the benefit of all, the following absence/tardy policies are in effect.\n\nSchool Hours\nStudents are to arrive at school the latest by 7:40 a.m. (5 minutes prior to the start of A-Block) and leave\nthe premises at 3:15 p.m. (5 minutes after the end of F-Block) unless they are meeting with a teacher for\nafter school assistance or are involved in an extracurricular activity supervised by school personnel. In the\nvirtual setting students should connect to the respective online classes 1-2 minutes before starting time.\nStudents in middle or high school will be marked tardy to any class if they arrive at the designated start\ntime for the period.\n\nAbsences\no Parents/guardians are asked to call the Secondary Office at 353-0808 or contact the division\nprincipal by email or WhatsApp on the day that their son/daughter is absent to request an\nexcused absence. Absence requests will be accepted up to 24 hours (1 day) after the absence.\n\no After an unforeseen absence of medical emergency (official certificates must be provided within\n24 hours of the absence (later notes will not be accepted) and upon the students return to\nschool, full credit will be given for missed work if it is completed within the time frame defined in\nthe late work policy (1-day-make-up time frame per day with up to 3 days for extended absences).\nCertificates from medical evaluations done in countries other than Bolivia must be presented\nwithin 24 hours upon return.\no Any work (including homework, quizzes, tests, papers, projects, oral presentations, etc.) not\ncompleted within the agreed upon time period after an absence will receive a grade of zero.\no Students who are absent two (2) or more of the five (5) academic periods on any given\ninstructional day, will be marked absent for the day.\no Students with more than 10 days of unexcused absence per school year will have to attend the\nWinter Academic Camp to earn credit in core subjects.\nReasons for excused absences must be either medical, sports related (national tournaments),\nmedical or a special family event involving someone in the immediate family.\n\no In the case of illness (more than 2 days off campus), notification from a doctor will be required. In\ncases of extended illness or major injury, arrangements approved by the school may satisfy\nattendance requirements (dealt with on a case by case basis).\no It is the responsibility of the students to schedule a date with the individual teacher to make up\nassessments missed as the result of an absence.\no Any major, long-term project or paper due on the day of an absence (assigned at least one week\nprior to the absence) should be delivered to the appropriate teacher on or prior to the due date\nor it will receive reduced or no credit (exception: absences due to medical emergency).\no Skipping Classes will be handled as a disciplinary matter. Students who skip class will receive zero\ncredit for the class work missed, tests or quizzes taken, and/or any homework due and will be\nsubject to disciplinary action depending upon the frequency of the behavior: parental contact,\nconversation with administration, make up hours being assigned, discipline letters and possibly\nsuspension.\no The Secondary Office will monitor absences and tardies and will notify parents and students\nwhen a trend toward an unacceptable increase of absences and/or tardies is detected. If the\nattendance problem does not improve after meeting with the parents, local authorities maybe\nnotified, In case of prolonged justified absences, the attendance of remediation classes will be\nmandatory.\no If a student is out during the school day for illness, etc.. he/she is not eligible to play or take part\nin any extracurricular activity that day.\n● The following are the only acceptable criteria for excused absences:\n\n○ Student sickness (2 days with parental note; more than 2 days with medical certificate).\n○ Serious illness of the student (medical certification from the specialist to be presented no\nlater than 48 hours upon return). Medical certificates need to be legible and include the\nexact diagnosis given for the ill student.\n○ Documented medical laboratory work or emergency doctor’s visit.\n○ Sport Tournaments (certification through athletic organization to be presented). Students\nwill not be excused for practices.\n○ Emergency in the family (parental note to be presented) such as medical emergencies of\nimmediate family members in case of accidents, terminal diseases and surgeries.\n○ Five days of excused absences for family events related to immediate family members\nsuch as weddings, birth of a sibling, graduations, etc.\n○ Civil unrest: cannot get to school due to unsafe conditions or road blockades.\n○ Participation in service events (coordinator´s note to be presented).\n○ Observance of a religious holiday (parental note to be presented).\n○ Extreme weather conditions that do not allow arriving at school (ie. significant flooding).\n○ External standardized tests (TOEFL, vocational testing, etc.).\n○ Prearranged college visits to foreign countries (documented by the university). Only email\nnotifications from the respective college or a college visit confirmation form or letter will\nbe accepted to excuse the absence. College visits will only be excused for SCCS students\nin grades 11 and 12. Maximum allowance for college visits is 5 school days.\n○ Documented Visa Interviews at embassies or consulates.\n○ Documented paper work (ID card, passport, driver’s license, etc.)\nWe encourage parents and students to program paperwork appointments or absences during vacations\nwhenever possible.\nImportant: Family trips and other absences of personal nature not included in the above list are\nconsidered unexcused absences. All unexcused absences will receive zero grades for in-class\nparticipation. Late work as a result of unexcused absences for personal reasons or any academic\nevaluation administered during the time of an unexcused personal absence (test, quiz, project), will\nreceive a deducted grade / be graded on a deducted scale: 20 percent daily for up to 3 days as stated in\nthe late work policy. It is entirely the student's responsibility to present make-up work and schedule\nmake-up exams.\n\nTardies / Early Departures\no Students who arrive late to school in the morning report to A-Block directly and will be marked\ntardy.\no Tardies will be reported and submitted on Plus Portal by the respective teacher. Students who\narrive late to the respective class will be marked tardy and may jeopardize their opportunity to\nfinish class assignments such as tests and quizzes in a timely manner.\no Parents are asked to call the Secondary Office by 7:40 a.m. if their son/daughter will be tardy to\nschool or will be departing early from school that day. No student will be allowed to leave\ncampus early without parental permission.\n\no Students who will be departing early must obtain an early dismissal exit pass from the Secondary\nOffice in order to leave class or campus. Exit passes are issued only upon receipt of a written note\nor a phone call from the parent (emails are acceptable).\no Four or more tardies in one week will result in a 2 hour Friday after school detention for any\nmiddle or high school student. If a student misses two Fridays of detentions, he/she will serve one\nday of external suspension. Parents will be notified by the secondary administration through\nemail or phone call, in case a student will have to attend on Friday.\nNote: Parents are reminded to arrange all travel plans well in advance and notify school administration\na minimum of five days before the absence.\n\nAbsences / Tardies for online classes (if we need to go to our online modality)\nFor a student's absence from online classes to be considered excused, parents need to contact the\nrespective principal to explain the reason that led to the absence (phone calls are acceptable). Parent and\nstudent emails to individual teachers will in no way create an excused absence. Absences and tardies from\nonline classes will be recorded and computed toward the student’s overall attendance record.\n\nLeaving Campus\nSCCS is a closed campus, meaning that students are not freely allowed to come and go over the course of\nthe school day. Students are required to present a pass to the school gate guard when they must leave\nthe campus during the school day. Early Dismissal passes are only issued by the Secondary School Office\nwhen requested in writing via email or through a phone call by the parents of the student who must\nleave. The request should include why and when the student must leave during class hours. (e.g. medical\nlab work).\n\nHomework During Absences\nIf a student is ill, he or she may call a classmate, check Plus Portal or Google Classroom or email their\nteachers with a copy to the Middle School Principal, High School Principal or Secondary Assistant Principal\nto get homework assignments. The Secondary School secretary is not responsible for collecting\nhomework assignments.\n\nWithdrawal Procedures\nAny student leaving SCCS must be properly checked out prior to departure from school. The checkout\nprocess is initiated by written parent notification to the Admissions Office. No student will be officially\nchecked out until all school obligations have been cleared and hardcopy textbooks, library books and\ninstruments returned. Official transcripts and school records will not be issued until the Business Office\nhas officially cleared a student.\n\nSchool Enrollment Certificates\nParents may require School Enrollment Certificates 24 hours in advance. A written request to the\nrespective Principal must indicate the name of the institution which requires the school certificate and\nthe language in which the certificate is requested.\n\nAttendance of school functions and school-related events\nParents, students, and teachers are expected to attend all important school events, parent meetings,\nschool functions, workshops, etc. In case a parent is unable to attend, a trusted family member should be\npresent. Please refer to the School Calendar for major events at SCCS. It is a requirement for all students\nto attend on-campus or virtual school assemblies. At least one parent per family is encouraged to attend\nat least two prevention program (Jaguar Parent University) activities a semester.\n\nField Trips /School Excursions\nIf conditions allow, any student participating in a field trip must have a field trip permission note signed by\nthe student's parent/guardian. This permission needs to be returned to the respective teacher by no later\nthan the day before the field trip takes place. Under no circumstances will a student be permitted to go\non the trip without the signed note. Authorizing participating via phone calls by parents will not be\naccepted. Only teachers, teaching assistants, parents of participating students, and students enrolled at\nSCCS will be permitted to participate in SCCS field trip activities. As of need interested parents will be\ninvited to field trips or school excursions.\n*Click Here to return to Top\n\nSTUDENT BEHAVIORAL EXPECTATIONS\nAs a school community, our priority is to assure a safe learning environment free from disruptions. Rules\nand regulations are established for the safety and well being of all students in our school. Failure to abide\nby school rules or to maintain appropriate conduct will result in disciplinary consequences. School rules\napply in school, on school grounds, on school buses and at school related activities. While the following\nrules are emphasized, they may not be all inclusive. Common sense and concern for safety of all shall\nprevail in situations where a rule has not been formalized. The purpose of disciplinary consequences is to\nhelp students internalize positive community values and ensure a safe learning environment for all.\nAppropriate conduct is expected at all times within the community. A student code of conduct has been\nwritten and all students sign at the start of the school year committing to an atmosphere of respect,\ninclusion and integrity.\n\nSecondary Dress Code (Grades 6-12)\nSecondary school boys are to wear blue jeans (blue or dark in color), SCCS track pants, track pants that\nare black with no writing or designs, SCCS issued & approved PE shorts with the SCCS collared shirt, SCCS\nPE Shirt or a white collared shirt with no emblems.\nSecondary school girls are to wear blue jeans (blue or dark in color), SCCS issued & approved PE shorts,\nJaguar leggings or leggings that are black with no writing or designs with the SCCS collared shirt, SCCS PE\n\nShirt or a white collared shirt with no emblems. Leggings with transparent parts (see-through leggings)\nmust cover legs down to the knees.\nAny jeans with rips above the knees are not permitted. Crocs, high heels, flip-flops, open-toe shoes and\nslippers are not allowed on campus. Caps or hats are not permitted in the academic classroom.\nOn Fridays students may also choose to wear activity shirts that represent the grade level, club or\nextracurricular activity that they are involved in as long as it carries the SCCS name. The design of all shirts\n(including SCCS Clubs, tournament jerseys and class shirts) must show the SCCS name/logo and be\napproved by the secondary administration prior to production. The administration reserves the right to\nadvise students and censor articles of clothing deemed inappropriate for school as well as revoke the\nright of wearing activity shirts on Friday if the dress code is not consistently followed by the student body.\nAt SCCS-sponsored events and activities students must wear either the SCCS student uniform or\nbusiness-like attire. Crop tops, shorts and miniskirts are not allowed. Wearing the official SCCS student\nuniform is not allowed at non-SCCS sponsored events and activities.\nWhile attending digital classes students are expected to wear their SCCS school uniform.\nStudents who are not in appropriate uniform will be sent to the division Principal where they will be\nrequired to call home and have the correct uniform brought to them. If a student is late to class as a\nresult, he/she will be marked tardy. If a student cannot have the correct uniform brought to them, they\nwill be provided with provisional clothing. Disciplinary consequences for not wearing the school uniform\ninclude:\n1. Parental notification and change of clothing (1st and 2nd offense)\n2. Parental notification, change of clothing and a Friday detention.\n\nStudent Behavior at School Assemblies\nStudents must avoid the display of any of the following behaviors during school assemblies and guest\nspeaker presentations:\n● Any physical contact with fellow students (hugging, punching, pushing, etc.)\n● Whistling or making inappropriate noises or comments\n● Wearing hats or caps, earphones or earbuds\n● Carrying or using water bottles, cups or any type of food\n● Carrying of backpacks and/or electronic devices\n● Putting feet on chairs or tables\n● Having conversations\n● Any body expression or posture that might be read as a lack of interest (incl. closing eyes for a\nlong period of time).\n\nPublic Displays of Affection (PDA)\nInappropriate displays of affection are to be avoided. Parents will be notified after an initial warning has\nbeen given and behavioral consequences will increase if the inappropriate actions continue.\n\nLockers & Backpacks\nIf lockers are available for use based on the current conditions, then proper care of lockers is the\nresponsibility of the students who use them. Students are assigned lockers to store backpacks, books and\nschool supplies. Students have to provide their own padlocks. Lockers must be locked at all times to\nprotect student property, school books and the locker door from damage. While lockers are issued to\nstudents at the beginning of the school year, they remain property of the school. The school reserves the\nright to check lockers and backpacks in the interest of the entire school community, or those of an\nindividual, for safety or health issues. If the student damages the school-issued locker, he or she must\npay for its replacement or repair. Locks left on the lockers at the end of the school year, will be removed.\n\nHall Passes\nAny student outside of their class during regular class time must always carry a Hall Pass provided by the\nrespective teacher. Students are required to return the Hall Pass to the teacher upon return to the\nclassroom. No more than one single student is allowed to leave the classroom at any given time (e.g. for\nrestroom visits, on-campus business, etc.). The student may never initiate visits to the school’s physician\nwithout having received permission from the respective teacher. If a student feels sick in class, the\nrespective teacher will provide the respective Medical Visitation Slip to be presented to the doctor's\noffice. The student must be accompanied by an adult or peer in order to ensure their safety. This note will\nbe signed by the physician with the exact time of return to class indicated. If a student feels sick during\nrecess or lunch time, he/she must report to class first in order to request authorization from the teacher\nto visit Health Services.\n\nCare of Grounds and School Property\nAll areas of the campus, especially the communal / social areas, are to be treated with respect at all\ntimes. Place litter in trash cans. Lunch boxes are to be removed from lunch tables at the end of lunch\ntime. It is every student’s obligation to help keep the SCCS campus clean and attractive, inside and outside\nthe classroom. Students should respect school property by not sitting on tabletops. SCCS students are\nrequired to respect school property at all times. Students are not allowed to approach or use a teacher’s\ncomputer. The use of a teacher’s computer will lead to immediate suspension. Students should not touch\nanything on a teacher’s desk. Damaging or destroying school property is considered a major school\nviolation.\n\nAcoustic Pollution / Music on Campus\nStudents are allowed to listen to music in class with the permission of the teacher and it must not be\ndisruptive to the learning environment. Special events on campus that are accompanied by music require\nspecial authorization by the respective division principal prior to the event. Screaming and shouting in the\nhallways, in the cafeteria or in the classrooms is strictly forbidden and will be handled as a disciplinary\nmatter. Headphones may be used in class only with permission by the classroom teacher. Students can\nnot be acoustically disruptive during recess and lunchtime and are not allowed to bring speakers to\ncampus without prior permission from the secondary administration.\n\nStudent Publications\nThe SCCS name or logo is not to be used in any written or digital publication without prior written\nauthorization of the respective school principal and the SCCS Director. Names that allude to SCCS such as\n“El Colegio Americano” or “El Americano” are not allowed to be used in any publication of events other\nthan those organized and sponsored by SCCS. SCCS is not responsible for a student’s content posted on\nthe internet during non-school hours. The school will inform parents and students when the school is\nmade aware of any inappropriate information being posted by an SCCS student on the internet and/or\nsocial media sites and, if necessary, inform local officials about the improper use of the internet and/or\nsocial media sites if it is deemed that it is putting another individual in any type of emotional and/or\nphysical danger. Students are not permitted to record, produce or distribute any viral videos (Tik Tok,\nSnapchat, Instagram, Youtube or any other social media website) on the school grounds without\nwritten authorization from the secondary administration. If the school administration is made aware of\nany unapproved viral video recorded, produced or distributed on campus, the respective disciplinary\nconsequences will apply and a written referral will be issued.\n\nSTUDENT RIGHTS AND OBLIGATIONS\nStudent Rights (Art. 33, Reglamento Interno de Funcionamiento)\nEach student has the right to:\n● Be secure and safe in his/her person and property;\n● Be treated with respect, courtesy and consideration by every student and adult within the school.\n● To receive academic instruction under safe and clean conditions and within the most excellent\ninfrastructure provided by SCCS.\n● To receive academic and professional orientation to achieve the best possible development at\npersonal, social and professional level, according to the student's abilities, aspirations or interests.\n● To be respected in terms of physical and moral integrity.\n● To receive respect for his/her religious or ideological convictions and personal integrity.\n● To be able to count on confidentiality by teachers and administrators regarding personal and\nfamily circumstances.\n● To not be discriminated against for reasons of birth, origin, gender, economic abilities, social\nstatus, religion, intellectual level or any given physical handicap.\n● To develop in a democratic learning environment and respectfully express concerns or make\nsuggestions to teachers and the secondary administration.\nStudent Obligations (Art. 34, Reglamento Interno de Funcionamiento)\nEach student at SCCS has the obligation to:\n● Attend classes with punctuality and participate in all academic activities organized by the school.\n● Be compliant with the schedule provided by SCCS and respect school hours.\n● Follow and respect teachers´ academic instructions.\n● Respect fellow students in their right to receive academic instruction.\n● Respect all members of the SCCS learning community.\n● Respect freedom of conscience and the religious and moral convictions, dignity and intimacy of all\nmembers of the SCCS learning community.\n\n● Not discriminate against any member of the learning community for reasons of birth, origin,\ngender, economic abilities, social status, intellectual level or any given physical handicap.\n● Care about, respect and make adequate use of the institution´s infrastructure and learning\nmaterials as well as the belongings of other members of the learning community.\n● Not interrupt academic instruction intentionally through jokes, disobedient or disruptive\nbehavior, etc.\n● Contribute with his/her behavior to a positive classroom environment.\n● Display at all times and occasions adequate performance in reference to personal appearance and\nbehavior and in compliance with the rules and regulations of the Ministry of Education and our\nschool.\n● Be compliant with any disciplinary consequence established as a result of confirmed misbehavior.\n● Return to classes on time after lunch/recess.\n● Display cautious behavior to prevent accidents on campus.\n● Place litter in trash cans around campus.\n● Maintain the school environment clean and organized.\n● Not leave the classroom without permission.\n● Be compliant with all academic and homework assignments.\nAll teachers and staff members in the school have authority over all SCCS students. Student behavior\nmust reflect self-respect, respect for others, and respect for learning.\n\nCLASSROOM VIOLATIONS\nStudents who choose to violate school and classroom rules will be subject to consequences. The severity\nof the violation and the student’s individual discipline record will be considered when assigning\nconsequences. This is applicable to the on-campus, the hybrid and the virtual classroom.\nIf a student has violated a school rule within the classroom, the teacher will initiate procedures to correct\nthe observed behavior. The teacher will work cooperatively with the student, parent and guidance staff\nto assist the student in understanding his/her responsibility for a change in conduct. The following\nconsequences may be assigned for inappropriate behavior:\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n\nVerbal warning to student\nStudent-Teacher Conference\nTelephone call or email home by the teacher\nTime Out (Recess Reflection Time or after-school reflection time with the teacher)\nWritten referral to Principal or Assistant Principal\nConference with student, parent and Principal or Dean of Culture following second call or email\nhome.\nAfter school reflection time or Saturday School assigned by the Principal or Assistant Principal\n(upon referral).\nPossible behavior agreement developed by Principal and signed by student, parent and Principal.\nPossible removal from class for a period of time (internal suspension)\nPossible removal from class and school environment (external suspension up to 10 days)\n\nDiscipline Referrals\nIf a change in behavior is not evident after classroom interventions by the teacher, a written discipline\nreferral will be made to the middle school principal, high school principal or secondary assistant principal.\nStudents who receive a referral will meet with an administrator to discuss the infraction, have their\nparents notified and receive an appropriate consequence. Parents must attend a meeting with the\nrespective school authority.\n\nSCCS STUDENT PHONE POLICY & GUIDELINES\nPhones are not to be used during school. Every student is assigned a personal Yondr Pouch. While the\nYondr Pouch is considered school property, it is each student’s responsibility to bring their Pouch with\nthem to school every day and keep it in good working condition. The use of cell phones is prohibited\nduring school hours (7:40 to 3:10 PM). Upon entering school, students need to turn off their cell phone\nand store it in a protective, sealed Yondr pouch which the student will keep on them throughout the day.\nAt the end of the day (3:10 PM) , students will be allowed to remove their cell from their Yondr pouch and\nuse their phone as needed.\nAny student seen with a cell phone during the day will have it confiscated and given to the secondary\nadministration. SCCS reserves the right to keep the student’s cell phone and cell phone chip during the\ntime of confiscation. No iWatches or other internet-connected watches can be worn during the school\nday.\nStudents are not allowed to take photos or make videos of anyone without parental permission.\nStudents may not take photos or make videos of staff members without their express permission.\nStudents are not allowed to send, share or forward potentially unwanted programs or harmful pieces of\nsoftware to fellow students or faculty members through any communication platform.\n\nDAILY PROCESS\nAs students Arrive to School, they will:\n1) Turn their phone off.\n2) Place their phone inside their Yondr Pouch and secure it in front of school staff.\n3) Store their Yondr Pouch in their backpack for the day.\nAt the end of the day, students will open their Yondr Pouch, remove their phone, close their Yondr\nPouch and put it in their backpack. Students must bring their Yondr Pouch to school with them each\nday.\n*Students arriving late or leaving early will pouch/unpouch their phones in the Secondary Office.\n\nVIOLATIONS\nUsing Phone During School- as stated in the handbook\n1. First offense: Teacher confiscates cell phone, delivers it to the Middle School Principal, Secondary\nSchool Assistant Principal or High School Principal and presents a written referral. The parents are\nnotified and may retrieve the electronic device after one week. (1st offense).\n\n2. Second offense: Teacher confiscates cell phone, delivers it to the respective Principal or Assistant\nPrincipal and presents a written referral. The parents are notified and may retrieve the electronic\ndevice after one month / 30 days (2nd offense).\n3. Third offense: Teacher delivers cell phone to the respective Principal/Dean of Culture and\npresents a written referral. The parents are notified and may retrieve the electronic device at the\nend of the semester. (3rd offense).\n\nPouch Damage / Lost Pouch\n1. First Offense: 30 US dollar replacement fee and cell phone policy applied\n2. Second Offense: Replacement fee, cell phone policy applied and consequences for damaging\nschool property.\n3. Third Offense: Replace fee, cell phone policy applied and referred to Teachers Council\nExamples of damage:\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n\nRipped\nCut\nTorn\nPen/pencil marks\nBent/cut pin\nSigns of force to black button on flap\n\nForgotten Pouch\nIf a student forgets their Pouch, their phone will be collected and Admin will call home to remind the\nParent of the policy. The phone will be returned to the student at dismissal.\nIf a student consistently forgets their Pouch, it is considered Lost. Refer to the Lost Pouch policy above.\n\nVIOLATIONS OF MAJOR SCHOOL RULES\nOne of the most important lessons to be learned is appropriate conduct. Self-control and consideration\nfor others are keys to good conduct. We expect all students to be accountable for their behavior at all\ntimes. Students, parents, and school staff members are jointly responsible for maintaining proper\nconduct in school, on school grounds, on school transportation and at school-related events.\nThe SCCS Community considers any of the following violations – on campus or during school sponsored\nactivities – a serious breach of our code of conduct:\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n\nAcademic Dishonesty\nFighting / Physical Assault\nInsubordination / Disrespect of school authorities.\nPossession, Sale or Use of Alcohol or Drugs on campus\nBeing intoxicated on campus\nBullying / Intimidation / Teasing / Inappropriate Gestures / Inappropriate Language (on campus,\nduring school sponsored activities or on cyberspace)\n● Forgery\n\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n\nPossession of Weapons (real or imitation).\nSmoking\nTheft\nVandalism\nVerbal, Written, Physical, Racial, Religious or Sexual Harassment\nStudent pranks or any activity of social disturbance that interferes with the nonviolent functioning\nof the secondary school.\n\nWhen students violate a major rule, our response is firm and immediate. The usual consequences for\nbreaking rules are as follows:\n●\n●\n●\n●\n\nWritten referral by teacher\nParental conference with Division Principal or Dean of Culture\nBehavior / Discipline Contract\nReflection time, in which the student must report to a designated place after school or on\nSaturdays (Saturday Reflection and Service Program) in order to work on a reflection paper or to\ncomplete other assigned tasks in a quiet environment.\n● Mandatory in-school and external counseling\n● The administration has the right to suspend a student initially for up to 3 days while an\ninvestigation of the incident takes place before a final punishment by the administration is\ndecided.\n● In case of a severe case, a teacher’s council will be convened and the school will follow the due\nprocess procedure mandated by the Bolivian Ministry of Education.\n○ Receive referral and any evidence\n○ Receive teacher/staff reports of the incident\n○ Doctor’s report if applicable\n○ Counselor’s report\n○ Principal’s report\n○ Principal’s report is received by the General Director\n○ Parents are informed of the incident in an in-person meeting\n○ Parents must give permission to interview their son/daughter\n○ Report by counselor of student’s involved or witnesses\n○ Teacher’s Council meets and reviews all reports and makes recommendations for any\nsanction\n○ Parents meet in person to be informed of the decision of the Teacher’s Council\n○ If necessary, the final report gets sent to the Defensoria de la niñez/adolescencia y\ndirección distrital de educación 2.\n● Internal suspension with zero participation grades, in which the student spends the entire day\nstudying in school but is not allowed to attend classes.\n● External suspension up to 10 school days with zero participation grades is applied in case of\nsevere behavior infractions such as:\n○ Repetition of three (3) simple behavior infractions\n○ Unexcused absences from school.\n○ Leaving the school campus without permission.\n○ Disrespectful and disobedient behavior toward SCCS personnel and faculty.\n\n○ Disrespectful behavior toward fellow students and other members of the SCCS\ncommunity (verbal expressions, gestures or written commentaries).\n○ Complicity in behaviors that violate the SCCS school rules.\n○ Misuse or destruction of school property or infrastructure (desks, walls, book shelves,\netc.)\n○ Religious verbal offenses or jokes.\n○ Disrespectful behavior toward symbols of Bolivia and the Cooperative.\n○ Repeatedly not bringing required school materials to school.\n○ Promote and start fights on school grounds or carry out physical aggression towards\nothers.\n○ Unauthorized absences from school functions.\n○ Any type of academic dishonesty.\n○ Any type of disqualifying comments and/or degrading behaviors that are not of\ndiscriminatory or racist nature.\n○ Any type of cyberbullying or cyber-violence against other SCCS students or the SCCS\nCommunity or the misrepresentation of SCCS through / misuse of an institutional email.\n○ Be on campus or at a school event under the influence of alcohol or drugs.\n○ Participate in dangerous or rude games.\n○ Create, initiate or publish material on websites, blogs, social or other media that\nconstitute disrespectful comments or offenses of SCCS and/or its members.\n○ Open, hide or damage other students’ backpacks or disrespect the belongings of fellow\nstudents.\n● Suspension for more than 10 school days or definitive expulsion from SCCS may be applied in\ncase of extreme (very severe) behavior infractions such as:\n○\n○\n○\n○\n○\n\n○\n○\n\n○\n○\n○\n○\n○\n\nRepeated severe behavior infractions.\nViolation of existing penal and educational laws.\nTheft or robbery of belongings of fellow students, teachers, or materials owned by SCCS.\nRepeated behaviors of bullying against members of SCCS (physical and/or sexual\naggression or harassment).\nCarrying, buying, selling and/or consumption/possession of alcoholic beverages,\ncigarettes, electronic cigarettes, narcotics, and controlled substances on school campus or\nat SCCS sponsored events.\n■ Possession\n■ Use\n■ Buying\n■ Selling\nCarrying of weapons or dangerous objects that can threaten or injure another person.\nDisplaying racist or discriminatory behavior or practices or degrading actions against\nstudents or other members of the cooperative school, in concordance with Law Nr. 045\nAgainst Racism and All Types of Discrimination.\nDiffusion of images that affect the privacy of SCCS students (male and female).\nPossession and diffusion of pornographic material in journals, videos, cell phones or other\ndevices or items on school grounds.\nForm or belong to delinquent gangs.\nCommitting fraud on academic evaluations, practice work and homework.\nFalsifying grades and/or signatures on academic evaluations, report cards, certificates,\nallowances and excuses as well as in agendas or notebooks.\n\nThe school may forward cases to the Teachers Council where serious or repeated and flagrant violations\nof the school rules have taken place. The Teacher’s Council, along with the administration, is the\nresponsible body for the implementation of severe disciplinary consequences, reporting severe\nmisconducts to the local educational authorities or recommending students for possible removal from the\nschool. The Director and School Board must review all cases whereby the student’s permanence at school\nis questioned. SCCS will follow the required reporting procedures to the designated competent\nauthorities with regards to the respective disciplinary infractions. LOCAL AUTHORITIES WILL BE NOTIFIED\nIN CASE OF ALL MAJOR DISCIPLINARY INFRACTIONS.\n\nPROPORTION OF SANCTIONS AT SCCS FOR POSSESSION, PURCHASE, SALE AND/OR\nCONSUMPTION OF CONTROLLED SUBSTANCES\nNational regulations indicate the following:\nMINISTERIAL RESOLUTION 001/2023\nArticle 98.- (Expulsion).\n.\n1. Within the framework of current regulations, related to the rights of girls, boys and adolescents,\nand the guarantees of due process, the expulsion of students from the Fiscal, Agreement and\nPrivate Educational Units without prior disciplinary process is prohibited, in accordance with Law\nNo. 548 of July 17, 2014, Child and Adolescent Code.\n2. When there is sufficient evidence of guilt, such as: robbery, theft, physical or sexual assault,\npurchase/sale or consumption or possession of alcoholic beverages, narcotics, controlled\nsubstances and weapons, and dissemination of images that affect the privacy of the students; as\nwell as racist, discriminatory and bullying practices or behaviors that constitute criminal offenses,\nthe involved student will be expelled, after a disciplinary process is carried out and the case is\nreferred to the competent authority.\n3. Likewise, the possession and dissemination of pornographic material in magazines, videos, cell\nphones or other media within educational units will be grounds for expulsion prior to disciplinary\nproceedings. Students are not allowed to interrupt academic studies and mothers, fathers or\nguardians are responsible for the continuity of academic studies.\n4. For the two previous paragraphs, the Directors of Educational Units, must send the records to\ncompetent authorities in the case of acts of violence, racism, discrimination, crimes or infractions\noutside the scope of internal regulations, filing the respective complaint of the internal\ndisciplinary process, where appropriate. All these documents must be sent together with a\ntechnical report to the corresponding Educational District Office.\n5. The Coexistence and Disciplinary Commission made up of all the representatives of the\nEducational Community within its functions, must carry out activities to prevent racist,\ndiscriminatory and bullying practices or behaviors that constitute criminal offenses.\n\nDISPOSITIONS OF THE SCCS STUDENT HANDBOOK\nA temporary expulsion for more than 10 days or a final expulsion of the student will be determined in\ncase of very serious offenses such as:\n\"... Possession, purchase, sale and/or consumption of alcoholic beverages, cigarettes, electronic\ncigarettes, narcotics and controlled substances within the establishment...\"\n\nThe school may refer the case to the Teachers' Council when there is a very serious offense or repeated\nand flagrant violations of the school rules. The Teachers' Council is the body responsible for determining,\ntogether with the school administration, the implementation of severe disciplinary measures, for\nreporting severe cases of misconduct to the educational authorities at the local level, and for\nrecommending a possible withdrawal from the school. The General Director and the Board of Directors\nwill review the cases in which the permanence of the student in the school is questioned.\n\nPENALTIES FOR THE POSSESSION AND/OR USE OF CONTROLLED SUBSTANCES:\nSuspension for three days initially, for the purpose of investigating the disciplinary incident involving the\npossession or use of a controlled substance, before deciding what final disciplinary action will be applied.\n● Conference with parents and the respective Director or Assistant Director.\n● External suspension for more than 10 days.\n● Commitment of Good Conduct signed by the student and her parents.\n● Mandatory attendance at sessions with a psychologist inside and outside the school.\n● Community work.\n● Other decisions made by the Teachers' Council.\nIt is an aggravating circumstance that the aforementioned offenses are committed by the student when\nhe is in a student exchange, representing the Cooperative in sports, cultural, scientific, social or other\nactivities. These offenses may also be penalized if they are committed outside the establishment by\nstudents wearing shirts and/or uniforms that identify the School.\nSANCTIONS FOR THE SALE OF CONTROLLED SUBSTANCES* on the SCCS campus and/or at SCCS\nsanctioned events\n(*as defined by Law 913 de La Lucha Contra el Tráfico Ilícito de Sustancias Contraladas (Law\nagainst the Sale & Traffic of Controlled Substances):\nSuspension for three days initially, for the purpose of investigating the disciplinary incident, before\ndeciding what final disciplinary action will be applied.\n● Definitive expulsion of the student, prior disciplinary process.\n● Send background and case reports to the District Office of Education.\n● File a complaint with the competent authority, since the sale of controlled substances is\nconsidered a criminal offense.\nProportional Sanctions Table\nCategory\n\nVapes / Cigarettes\n\nAlcohol\n\nControlled Substances\n(including medication\nwithout prescription)\n\nPossession on campus\n& at school events\n\n3 day external\nsuspension\n\n5-day external\nsuspension\n\nmore than 10-days of\nexternal suspension\n\nUse on campus & at\n\n5-day external\n\n10-day external\n\nDefinitive Expulsion\n\nschool events\n\nsuspension\n\nsuspension\n\nSale on campus &/or at\nschool events\n\n10-day external\nsuspension\n\n10-day external\nsuspension\n\nDefinitive Expulsion\n\nThe SCCS Prevention Program\nSCCS is committed to provide a proactive approach to intervention and parent guidance. Intervention\nworkshops are tailored to specific group/grade level needs along with a range of general school\ncounseling workshops. These workshops are designed to address a variety of issues and support students\nin their academic, personal, and social development and provide students with practical information and\nstrategies. You can read a more comprehensive description of our program here.\nSome of the topics covered with students and parents throughout the year are:\n● Stress Management: Our stress management workshops teach students practical strategies for\nmanaging stress and maintaining their emotional well-being.\n● Study Skills: Our study skills workshops provide students with tips and strategies for improving\ntheir study habits, staying organized, and achieving academic success.\n● Social Skills: Our social skills workshops help students develop the communication and\nrelationship-building skills they need to be successful in all areas of their lives.\n● Anger Management: Our anger management workshops help students understand the root\ncauses of anger, develop effective coping strategies, and maintain positive relationships with\nothers.\n● Substance Abuse Prevention: Our substance abuse prevention workshops provide students with\ninformation on the dangers of substance abuse, and strategies for avoiding drugs and alcohol.\n● Mental Health Awareness: Our mental health awareness workshops provide students with\ninformation on common mental health issues, such as depression and anxiety, and how to access\nsupport and resources.\nThe above workshops are also part of the Jaguar Parent Workshops which typically consists of quarterly\nworkshops provided by both the School Counselor and external professionals (Protege tu Corazon or\nother local Psychologists with specific expertise).\nAs part of the institutional prevention measures, SCCS reserves the right to perform alcohol testing\nwith a breathalyzer, student backpack and locker searches, drug-testing with parental permission and\ninstall detection devices in strategic areas of the school campus.\n\nFair & Due Process\nIn order to suspend a student there is a four-part process that the school must follow.\nThe school must:\n\n(1) Gather evidence / written testimony relating to the incident from ALL parties involved.\n(2) Meet with the student and counselor to review the incident and the evidence gathered.\n(3) Notify the parent and student explaining the reason for the suspension.\n(4) A suspension may require a review of the case by the Teacher’s Council.\n\n(5) A definitive expulsion will require a review of the case by the Teacher’s\n\nCouncil.\n\nExtenuating and Aggravating Circumstances\nIn all disciplinary matters, the administrator has the right to apply a more severe or less severe\npunishment based upon the actions of the individual involved in the incident.\n\nBehavior / Discipline Agreements\nThis is a written document that establishes responsibilities and commitments that the student must meet\nas well as potential consequences for lack of compliance. The agreement will be in effect for a defined\nperiod of time that is established by the Division Principal and is supported by the faculty/staff. The\nstudent, his/her parents and the Division Principal sign this agreement. Students may also be given a\ndisciplinary or academic agreement as a condition of their registration or acceptance at the school. In this\ncase, the student will be required to meet the conditions of the contract within the specified time.\n\nClarification on Academic Dishonesty\nThe following actions are considered academic dishonesty:\n1. Copying from someone else’s paper or divulging test information.\n2. Copying another's homework or allowing someone to copy one's own homework.\n3. Plagiarism (to steal or pass off the ideas or written work of another as one's own).\n4. Purposely missing school or being late to avoid a test or paper.\n5. Presenting work as your own when helped by others, including parents or tutors,\npublished material or any internet source/webpage or Artificial Intelligence platforms\n(please see the SCCS AI policy).\n6. Recycling of academic materials: Presentation of the same or similar work assignment in\ndifferent classes.\n7. Possessing tests / exams without permission of the teacher.\n8. Not following the SCCS test taking policy.\n9. Accessing the teacher’s grade book or using the teacher’s computer.\n10.Consciously completing work assignments for other students, including the reception of\nremuneration to complete or write assignments for other students.\n\nPlagiarism\nPlagiarism is the use of another person’s ideas, “facts” or original work without giving proper credit (ex.\nfootnotes, endnotes). To avoid plagiarizing, be sure to do the following:\no If you copy a quote, footnote it.\no If you paraphrase, however loosely, you must footnote.\no In general, if you borrow an idea from someone else, you must give that person credit.\n\nCommon Knowledge\nIf a fact is common knowledge, then no footnote is needed. Common knowledge is on the level of the\nfact that Neil Armstrong was the first man to set foot on the moon in 1969. If you have the slightest\n\ndoubt whether or not to footnote something, footnote it. If you footnote common knowledge it is fine. If\nyou fail to footnote something you should, you are plagiarizing.\n\nHomework\nStudents are encouraged to discuss the concept of “collaboration” with individual teachers. An example\nof cheating might be copying the work of a fellow student, in which case the Division Principal or the\nDean of Culture shall be notified immediately and disciplinary action will be taken accordingly.\n\nConsequences of Academic Dishonesty\nThe zero received on work that is involved, in a case of cheating, is not part of the punishment.It is merely\nthe assessment of the value of the work. Disciplinary consequences for academic dishonesty are the\nfollowing:\n\nFirst Incident:\n● No suspension, a resubmission of the work assignment with reduced grade (no higher than a 60)\nand, the incident filed on their permanent record, not eligible for academic recognition, put on\nprobation if a member of any SCCS extracurricular programs and a parental meeting. (1st offense)\n\nSecond Incident:\n● A zero on the work assignment, the incident filed on their permanent record, prohibited to\nparticipate in extracurricular activities for the semester and a parental meeting. (2nd offense).\n\nThird Incident:\n● One day of external suspension, a zero on the work assignment, the incident filed on their\npermanent record, prohibited to participate in extracurricular activities for the rest of the school\nyear and a parental meeting (3rd offense).\nSubsequent incidents of academic dishonesty will be referred to the teacher council for recommendations\non for further disciplinary actions.\n\nAcademic Dishonesty in Online Courses\nStudents enrolled in an authorized online academy are expected to observe the code of academic honesty\nrequired of the students enrolled in this program. Any compromise of this Academic Honesty policy,\nwhether it involves copying a homework assignment, cheating on a test, or plagiarizing written responses,\nwill be treated seriously by the teachers and the administration at the respective academy. If a student\ncommits an act of academic dishonesty in any of the online courses enrolled, SCCS will not be recognizing\nthe respective course credit.\n\nSCCS Test Taking Policy for Middle and High School\nThis policy applies to all standardized tests administered at SCCS Secondary School (MAP, WrAP, Pre-ACT,\nACT, SAT, ACT, etc.) and exams administered in academic classes.\n\n1. Students are expected to arrive at the testing location on time. Late-arrivals will result in\nthe loss of the opportunity for the individual student to be admitted to the respective test\nand receive test credit (on standardized tests) and will jeopardize the student's\nopportunity to finish tests in a timely manner (on SCCS tests).\n2. Students are expected to bring all required materials to the testing session\n(subject-specific materials required, Nr. 2 pencils, pens, sharpener, eraser, authorized\ncalculators, reading materials, etc.)\n3. Restroom visits are to be done before, not during a testing session, except in case of\nunavoidable emergencies.\n4. All personal belongings should be placed in a secure area inside the testing room or in an\narea designated by the teacher/proctor. Only items required by the teacher/proctors\nshould be in sight on the desk.\n5. Any electronic devices including watches, unless used for test purposes, must be left with\npersonal belongings and must be turned off. During standardized test administrations\nelectronic devices will be collected and stored by the test proctor until all test-takers have\ncompleted the exam. Only calculators approved by school or testing agencies may be\nused for exams.\n6. Verbal and non-verbal conversations are not allowed during academic tests.\n7. During exams students must be facing the front of the testing room at all times.\nUnauthorized changes in seating positions, lying down with heads on testing tables or\nplacing legs on chairs or tables is not allowed.\n8. Students must follow all exam instructions throughout the entire testing session and stop\nresponding to exam questions when the end of the exam section or exam period is\nannounced.\n9. Students must turn in all exam materials before leaving the exam room.\n10.Students will be suspended from the exam and be awarded a “0”-grade under the\nfollowing circumstances:\n■ a student appears to be talking or signaling answers;\n■ a student appears to be gazing at another student's work;\n■ a student neglects to protect his/her answer sheet from view of classmates;\n■ a student uses unauthorized materials or devices during an exam;\n■ a student displays any other type of suspicious behavior that indicates an act of\nacademic dishonesty.\n11.All students are required to keep their attention on their own work.\n12.Allegations of academic dishonesty will be referred to the Principal for investigation and\ndisciplinary intervention.\n13.Under no circumstances, exam or quiz questions can be shared among students. The\nsharing and reception exam questions without the permission of the respective teacher\nis considered academic dishonesty.\n\nItems not allowed on school campus:\nSlime - weapons or fireworks – lighters/matches – shoe polish – spray-paint (without teacher permission)\n- hats/caps in classrooms or during assemblies – carnival shirts (casacas) and any other carnival related\nitems.\n\nWeapons\nThe possession or the use of flammable materials (lighters, liquids, matches), any kind of explosive\nmaterials/fireworks, or any kind of weapon (or weapon parts) on school grounds can lead to immediate\nsuspension and/or referral of the student to the Teacher Council. No weapons of any type, including\ndefensive weapons such as pepper spray, are allowed on campus. Bolivian Law requires those materials to\nbe confiscated and an incident report to be sent to district authorities.\n*Click Here to return to Top\n\nExtracurricular Program / Extracurricular Activities\nSCCS offers its students the opportunity to participate in a variety of activities beyond the traditional\nclassroom. Students are able to pursue an area of personal interest while building unique relationships\nwith teachers and students. Several after-school activities will be offered to SCCS secondary students in\nthe 2025-26 school year: Team Sports, StuCo (6-12), NJHS (7-9), NHS (10-12), Operation Smile (8-12),\nManitos, Hands Helping Hands, TECHO, Environmental Awareness Club (8-12), MUN and others. The SCCS\nExtracurricular and Service Club policy guides these clubs to ensure inclusiveness and good practice.\n\nAthletics Program\nThe sports program includes Junior Varsity and Varsity teams for all sports offered; students aged 11 – 14\nare eligible for Junior Varsity and aged 15 – 18 for Varsity teams. For additional information, schedules\nand specific Athletics Department policies, rules and expectations regarding student participation in the\nsports program, please contact the SCCS Athletic Department.\nAll students who maintain good academic and conduct standing are eligible to participate in the\nextracurricular activities sponsored by SCCS. Any student with academic or attendance problems will be\nineligible to participate (see Grades and Athletic Eligibility).\n\nStudent Athletes Policy\n● Student-athletes who frequently participate in off-campus tournaments are still required to\nattend on-campus classes, if possible, when they are not directly playing or actively engaged in\ntheir sport.\n● As a part of the absence paperwork, the tournament schedule or invitation with dates and\nlocation is to be presented to the administration before the respective absence can be\nauthorized.\n● During the times of absences due to athletic tournaments students have one day extra for every\nday they miss school with a maximum of 3 days to have all assignments turned in. Tests and\nquizzes are required to be scheduled for make-up within three days of return to classes.\n● During the times of absences due to athletic tournaments, student athletes are required to stay in\npermanent email contact with the teachers and access google classroom daily.\n● Student athletes who are absent for standardized tests are required to make these up (if\nscheduling permits).\n● In order to receive authorization for travel or tournament participation, student athletes must\nhave passing grades in all subjects (no F-grades) and an overall grade point average of 70 percent.\n\n● Students with a referral for a major violation of school policy (including Academic Dishonesty) are\nnot eligible for athletic participation.\n● A student with only one failing grade of a 59 and an overall GPA of a 70 percent is eligible for an\nathletic eligibility contract issued by the division principal.\n● Tournament invitations must be presented to the administration before the athletic absence can\nbe authorized.\n\nSecondary Student Council\nStudents elected to the Student Council (StuCo officers and class reps) will use an array of different skills:\nmovie making, research, communication, marketing, and networking. StuCo members will take initiative\nand positive leadership roles in the school and community. The Student Council is expected to have an\nactive and visual presence in the 5-26 school year. Videos about STUCO initiatives will be created and\nshared with the SCCS community. StuCo will also be raising awareness about thematic ideas such as\nWomen’s International Day, Earth Day, Bullying Awareness Month and much more. Students who want to\nhave a positive impact on fellow students, and make them think not only about themselves, but also\nBolivia and the world, are welcome to support StuCo.\n\nNational Junior Honor Society\nThe National Junior Honor Society is the United States’ premier organization established to recognize\noutstanding middle level students (7-9th grade). More than just an honor roll, NJHS serves to honor those\nstudents who have demonstrated excellence in the areas of scholarship, leadership, service, citizenship\nand character. These characteristics have been associated with membership in the organization since its\nbeginning in 1929.\n\nNational Honor Society\nThe National Honor Society (NHS) is the nation's premier organization established to recognize\noutstanding high school students. More than just an honor roll, NHS serves to recognize those students\nwho have demonstrated excellence in the areas of scholarship, leadership, service, and character. Today, it\nis estimated that more than one million students participate in NHS activities. NHS chapters are found in\nall 50 US states, the District of Columbia, Puerto Rico, many U.S. territories, and Canada. Chapter\nmembership not only recognizes students for their accomplishments, but challenges them to develop\nfurther through active involvement in school activities and community service.\n\nApplying for NJHS or NHS\nStudents are invited to apply for membership to NJHS or NHS at the start of the school year based on\ntheir grades, attendance and conduct from the previous year. The selection process of NJHS/NHS\nmembers is regulated by US-established NJHS/NHS guidelines and carried out by the NJHS or NHS advisor\nin coordination with the NJHS or NHS Faculty Council. Students must demonstrate a minimum average of\n93 percent (no grade below a 75 and only 1 C), no disciplinary incidents, no more than 20 tardies or 3\nunexcused absences in a semester and must be approved by the Faculty Council in order to gain\nmembership. When accepted, members are expected to maintain high academic standards, serve as\n\npositive role models to their peers and participate in a minimum of 6 hours of community service per\nquarter.\n\nService Clubs - Leadership Regulations\nEach of the SCCS Service and Extracurricular Clubs (OS, HHH, TECHO, MANITOS, MUN, etc.) provides\nstudent leadership opportunities through one of the officership positions: President, Vice-President,\nTreasurer, Secretary, Historian, Social Media Coordinator. In order to be nominated for the positions of\nclub president or club vice-president the respective student must have been a member in good standing\nin the institution and approved by both the sponsor and administration. The social media coordinator for\neach club must coordinate with Mrs. Daniela Canessa and receive prior approval from the administration\nfor publication and distribution.\nThe following aspects must be taken into account when a club president or vice-president are selected:\nnumber of years in the organization/club, number of club-related events attended, merits and\nrecognitions received throughout the time in the org. If elected president or vice-president of a school\nclub, a student is not eligible to become president or vice-president of another club or institution.\n\nClass Activities and Fundraising\nSpecial class or club activities such as bake-sales, fundraising, expositions, field-trips, etc. are to be\nauthorized one week in advance by the respective Principal. An activity authorization form can be\nretrieved from the Secondary Office. Activity Authorization Forms need to be signed by the teacher and\nhave to be returned to the Principal’s Office for approval. Students may not sell products for personal gain\non school grounds at any time.\n\nPolicy for Creating a new extracurricular club or activity\nAt Santa Cruz Cooperative School (SCCS), we encourage student initiative and leadership through the\nformation of extracurricular clubs and activities that enhance our learning community and align with our\nschool’s mission. Students who wish to propose a new club must follow the guidelines below:\n1. Faculty Sponsorship:\nEach club must have a designated faculty sponsor who agrees to support, supervise, and guide\nthe club’s activities.\n2. Inclusivity:\nClubs must be open to all secondary students regardless of grade level, background, or ability.\nMembership must promote a welcoming and inclusive environment.\n3. Mission & Purpose:\nThe club must have a clearly stated goal or mission that outlines its focus and intended activities.\nProposals should demonstrate how the club supports the SCCS mission: to cultivate lifelong\nlearners who are critical and creative thinkers, principled leaders, and compassionate global\ncitizens.\n\n4. Proposal Submission:\nStudents must submit a written proposal by the end of the second full week of either the first or\nsecond semester to the Secondary Leadership Team including:\n○ Club name\n○ Purpose/mission statement\n○ Proposed activities or meeting plans\n○ Name of faculty sponsor\n○ List of at least five interested student members\n5. Approval Process:\nThe Secondary Leadership Team will review proposals to ensure alignment with SCCS values and\napprove new clubs accordingly.\nAll approved clubs must operate with integrity, reflect our community standards, and contribute\npositively to the student experience at SCCS.\n*Click Here to return to Top\n\nSTUDENT SERVICES\nHealth Service\nThe school doctor is available for treatment of minor mishaps, accidents and consultations. A Medical\nVisitation Pass MUST be secured from a teacher referring the student to the doctor’s office at all times. If\nthe doctor is not present, the student must return to class or in case of emergency, report to the\nPrincipal’s office. Questions regarding health care and insurance are best directed to the SCCS physician\non duty. Parents are expected to take their child to a private physician in case of illness.\nIf a student needs to take medication during the school day, the parent is expected to inform the doctor\nand provide the medication to the doctor who will administer the medication during the school day.\nParents are expected to purchase the emergency medical insurance that the school offers during\nregistration. This allows the school the ability to provide ambulance and emergency services quickly and\nefficiently if there happens to be an accident on campus.\n\nSchool Visitors\nAny visitor on campus must register with the security personnel and have a scheduled appointment with\nan SCCS staff member.\n\nGuest Speakers\nGuest speakers’ invited to SCCS require prior authorization of their visit by the Director General and\nrespective Principal and have to present their biographic/professional résumé and presentations prior to\nthe visit.\n\nTelephones\nSchool office telephones are for business purposes. In emergency situations, students may be allowed to\nuse an office telephone if granted permission. Routine calls home for forgotten homework and rides after\nschool, etc. will not be permitted on school phones. The use of cellular phones on campus is restricted to\nbefore 7:40 a.m. and after 3:15 p.m. Use of cell phones at any other time will result in an electronic\ndevice referral and cell phone confiscation.\n\nTechnology Department\nThe technology department provides equipment and services for secondary school students. Each\nstudent, upon signing his or her Acceptable Use Policy (see Appendix B) will receive network and Plus\nPortal login information. In addition, SCCS faculty posts course information on the school's Plus Portal\nwebsite. Questions regarding technology at SCCS will be best directed to Mr. Carlos Paredes, Technology\nCoordinator and iPad Program Coordinator (only if related to these programs).\n\nComputer Rights and Responsibilities\nAs our instructional technology is a limited resource, SCCS believes that the sole use of that resource\nshould be to further the educational goals of the school. The following guidelines are intended to help\nstudents understand appropriate use. Failure to respect these guidelines could result in immediate\nrestriction or termination of access and/or other disciplinary action.\n\nPrivileges of SCCS network users\no The right to privacy in your electronic communication and work. SCCS recognizes and respects the\nneed for privacy when it comes to data stored on the network and sent through the school’s\nemail server. It should be noted, however, that the computers and all data in them are school\nproperty. Because the school acts as a “host” for your account, it has a responsibility to ensure\nthe legality of all behavior conducted in its name. There are occasions when a member of the\ntechnology staff may need to access personal email or files for security or maintenance reasons.\no Equal access to network services. All members of the SCCS community have the right to equal\naccess to the school’s resources. Some uses, such as classroom work or homework, have priority\nover other uses, such as email or “web surfing”. No single user should monopolize a computer or\nthe network.\n\nResponsibilities of SCCS network users\n● Never share your password or account with anyone. You have full responsibility for the use of\nyour account and will be held responsible for any policy violations traced to your account.\n● Do not knowingly degrade the performance of the network. For example, electronic chain letters\nand Internet chat rooms are prohibited for this reason. Downloading and/or storage of\nnon-classroom material from home or the Internet is forbidden and will result in the loss of\nnetwork access. Games are not allowed.\n● Honor all rules of copyright and personal property. Ownership of text, music, software and other\nmedia must be respected. This prohibits the copying of software and, in particular, games to or\nfrom school computers.\n● Avoid the knowing or inadvertent spread of computer viruses.\n● Use appropriate language and behavior. Profanity or obscenity is not permitted at any time. As\nthe computer labs are shared workspaces, be courteous and polite in the computer labs as well.\n“Hacking” or altering the operating system or programs on school computers is unacceptable and\nconsidered a very serious violation of school rules.\n● Impersonation, anonymity, and pseudonyms are not permitted. As an educational network, we\nbelieve that individuals must take responsibility for their actions and words. Use of another’s\nlogin, even if authorized by that person, is expressly forbidden.\n● Use of the network for illegal or unethical activities is prohibited. Such activities include\ntampering with computer hardware or software, unauthorized entry into computers, or\nknowledgeable destruction of computer files.\n● Respect school policies. As SCCS considers computers an academic research tool, students may\nalso lose computer privileges for failure to respect technology regulations at SCCS.\n● Sensible use of natural resources. Please avoid the unnecessary printing and throwing away of\npaper.\n● Responsible use of email. Email accounts are to be accessed for school communication and\nwork. Spam and other irresponsible use of the system are forbidden. Students with questions\nabout acceptable use of email should contact a member of the technology department for\nfurther details.\nSCCS Internet Privacy Policy\nSCCS is strongly committed to protecting the privacy of students and families. We feel it is important to\nprovide a secure environment for students at school and to ensure that sensitive information is not made\navailable to the outside world. At the same time, we recognize the power of the Internet to communicate\ninformation to the SCCS community as well as prospective families and teachers. In an effort to provide\nthe best information possible, the school reserves the right to publish candid and random photographs of\nstudents and teachers throughout the school’s web site and the SCCS Facebook page. If you you do not\nwant your child’s picture published on any SCCS platforms, please provide this information via email or\nin person to Ms. Daniela Canessa at dcanessa@sccs.edu.bo\nSCCS is not responsible for a student’s content posted on the internet during non-school hours. The\nschool will inform parents and students when the school is made aware of any inappropriate information\nbeing posted by an SCCS student on the internet and when necessary inform local officials about the\nimproper use of the internet if it is deemed that it is putting another individual in any type of danger\nemotionally or physically.\n\nCafeteria\nBalanced and nutritious meals are provided every day at our cafeteria or other assigned eating areas. If\nthe conditions allow, hot lunches may be served by an external catering service. If students choose to use\nthe catering service, parents must purchase tickets at the window by the Barcelona gate or directly from\nthe provider. Students may also opt to bring their own lunch from school. ALL STUDENTS ARE REQUIRED\nTO EAT AT SCHOOL. Students are expected to clean up and sanitize their space prior to leaving the tables\nin order to leave the tables clean for those who follow. Students are asked to bring a snack to school, and\nare provided time in their schedule to eat it. Students are to eat the food served in their designated space\nor bring their lunchbox from home. Additional catering services may not have food delivered within the\nschool gates. The use of a delivery service (PedidosYa, Yango, etc.) is not permitted on campus as SCCS\ncannot be held responsible for what is in the bag. Food delivered by any of these services to campus\nwill not be received. The secondary office will not make phone calls for students to order food. Any\nbehavior that interferes with the proper functioning of the cafeteria or the respective eating areas is\nstrictly forbidden and will be handled as a serious disciplinary matter.\n\nLibrary/Media Center\nThe Library Media Center is equipped with literature, reference books, subscriptions to magazines, several\ncomputer workstations and several iPads with access to Internet and access to search databases. It is\nstaffed by a professional librarian plus an assistant. The library is a place for students to secure resources\nand leisure reading materials and to do research. The library is open Monday to Thursday from 7:50 a.m.\nto 4:00 p.m. and Friday from 7:50 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\nThe basic library rules are:\no All students must have a pass from a teacher to use the library during class periods.\no The Library is a FOOD FREE SPACE (no food, gum, candy water, soda etc.).\no Keep the noise level to a minimum.\no Printing at the Library Media Center requires the use of a copy card. Copy cards can be purchased\nin advance at the business office (which is open 8-12:15 and 1-4; it may be a good idea to plan\nahead and purchase print cards before you need it at the last minute).\nFailure to comply with library rules will result in immediate removal from the library. Subsequent\noffenses will result in a suspension of library services. Any disciplinary offense in the library will be\nreported and sanctioned according to the SCCS discipline policy.\n\nSCCS Library Lending Policy for Students\n● Books from the regular collection and periodicals may be checked out for a period of 2 weeks. If\nyou have overdue materials, you will not be able to check out additional materials until overdue\nmaterials are returned.\n● DVDs are maintained for curriculum support and may be checked out by teachers.\n● Material may be renewed any time unless there has been a patron request.\n● Patrons with overdue material may not borrow additional material.\n● Overdue notices will be sent to students once a quarter.\n\n● All patrons will pay for lost or damaged material. Lost books are paid for at the dollar cost of\npurchase and importation. Lost or damaged books may be replaced with an exact copy (not\nphotocopy) in good condition, in place of the “lost book fee”.\n● At the end of each semester, library material more than one month overdue or not returned will\nbe considered lost. The replacement cost will be charged to the student. If a student owes\noutstanding library charges, no transcript, letter of recommendation or report card will be issued\nuntil the charges have been paid in the Business Office.\n● If a “lost” item was paid for and is subsequently found and returned to the library in good\ncondition, the patron's money will be refunded.\n\nLost and Found\nA place for valuable lost and found items is maintained in the Secondary Office. If you find an item that\ndoes not belong to you, give it to an administrator, a teacher or a secretary immediately. Sports\nequipment is to be turned into the P.E. Department. DO NOT BRING VALUABLES TO SCHOOL! DO NOT\nBRING LARGE AMOUNTS OF MONEY TO SCHOOL! Video recordings of the security camera system are not\na source to identify and find lost items at SCCS. Only under special circumstances (suspicion of theft, etc.)\nwill the Division Principal grant authorization to specialized school personnel to revise security recordings.\n\nSecurity and Emergency Drills\nSafety route maps are posted in each classroom. Emergency drills are required by law and are an\nimportant safety precaution. Students must quietly evacuate the building under the supervision of their\nteacher. Students at lunch should exit the cafeteria and move to the athletic field, where they must report\nto their pre-assigned locations.\n\nSports Complex\nSCCS has an extensive sports complex, including a gymnasium, a covered court, a mat and weight room\nand two soccer fields. If the conditions allow, these facilities are used for physical education classes and\ninterscholastic sports, and for certain SCCS community events. Students must have permission from the\nathletic department to use the coliseum outside of any regularly scheduled class. Students may not invite\nstudents from other schools to use our facilities without permission from the P.E. Department.\n\nRegulations for Use of Facilities\n● Students cannot be on campus after school hours or on non-school days without adult\nsupervision.\n● Students will not be permitted on the facility grounds unless the government permits it and\nunless strict biosecurity measures are followed.\n● It is the student’s responsibility to clean the cafeteria and jatata tables at the end of recess and\nlunch hours. All lunch boxes need to be picked up at and returned to the Barcelona gate at the\nbeginning and end of the break/lunch period. DO NOT LEAVE LUNCH BOXES ON THE JATATA\nTABLES.\n● During events occurring after school hours or non-school days, participants and spectators are\nexpected to remain in or near the area where the event takes place.\n● Animals are not allowed on campus.\n\n● SCCS is a smoke-free campus.\n● The use of campus facilities by a group or individual must be approved in advance by the Director\nor his designee.\n● During school hours, Secondary School students may only go into Elementary School areas with\nspecial authorization.\n\nSecurity for Personal Items\nStudents are assigned a locker at the start of the school year and are encouraged to use it for their\npersonal items. Students are expected to bring a personal combination lock to secure their locker. Please\ndo not bring large sums of money or other valuables to school. Students are responsible at all times for\npersonal items, including their electronic devices.\n*Click Here to return to Top\n\nParent Involvement and Communication\nParent-Teacher Communication\nParents will have the opportunity to meet their child’s teachers at the start of the school year at the\nannual Back to School Night. Communication via email is encouraged between parents and students and\nwhen necessary, a personal appointment can be made through the secondary office. Each teacher has\ntwo designated times weekly to meet with parents.\n\nMother’s Club\nSCCS is fortunate to have a Mother’s Club made up of elected delegates at each grade level. The\nMother’s Club provides support at school throughout the year in a variety of extracurricular activities, as\nwell as helping to plan field trips, guest speakers and helping to find academic resources to enhance the\ncurriculum. There is a meeting at the start of the year where the teachers have the opportunity to meet\nthe delegates from each grade level.\n\nStudent Led Conferences (6-12)\nParents are entitled to request conferences with teachers at all times throughout the school year; weekly\nparent-teacher conference times will be published at the beginning of each year. Report cards and\nstandardized testing results will be issued during two formal parent conferences in October and March.\nAttendance by at least one parent to these conferences is obligatory. It is the parents´ responsibility to\nstay informed about the student's academic progress and sign all pertinent school documents (progress\nreport, report cards, parent-teacher-conference docs, etc.). It is important for parents to be an active part\nof the educational process. We recommend that parents communicate regularly with teachers (attend\nstudent study team meetings, be aware of progress reports, visit the Plus Portal website and attend\nconferences) to converse about the student's academic and social progress.\n*Click Here to return to Top\n\nTransportation and Parking\nDrop-off and Pick-up Policies\nStudents should be dropped off to school between 7:15 to 7:40 each morning at the secondary entrance\non Calle Barcelona. We ask that you quickly drop off your child so that traffic continues to flow smoothly.\nStudents are available for pick-up starting at 3:10 each day and the guards will assist by calling the\nstudent’s name when the parent pulls up to the entrance.\n\nParking Regulations\nThere are designated parking spots for parents on Calle Barcelona but may fill quickly if there is an event\non campus. If these spots are filled, the area across from the school is often opened up and a fee is\ncharged for parking.\n\nTransportation for School Events\nSCCS has three buses that are used to provide students with transportation to athletic events or field\ntrips. No student is allowed to ride on a bus without an authorized permission slip signed by a parent or\nguardian.\n*Click Here to return to Top";
